1795187018084948	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3up	{"description":"Seen in the 2026-11-20 review, not tested. The filter is an nftables table in the sandbox's network namespace, so it only governs families that live in a network namespace. Nothing (no seccomp filter, no Landlock rule) stops the command from creating sockets of other families. AF_VSOCK is the one that matters: on kernels where vsock is global rather than per namespace, a sandboxed command on a machine with VMs (or inside a guest) could reach guest or host vsock listeners, and with vsock_loopback a listener on the same host (CID 1). Not reproduced here: no vsock module is loaded (lsmod empty, /proc/sys/net/vsock absent) and creating the socket would autoload a kernel module on the host, which the review was not allowed to cause. To do: on a machine with vsock loaded, run a host listener and try from inside --no-network; check the kernel's vsock namespace mode (newer kernels have net.vsock ns_mode). Options: (a) a seccomp filter in stage X that allows socket() only for AF_UNIX, AF_INET, AF_INET6, AF_NETLINK (cost: first seccomp use in the project, an allow-list that must be kept right, programs using other families break); (b) doctor check plus a refusal to start when vsock is loaded and global (cost: refuses on every VM host); (c) document as residual. Bluetooth sockets are refused by the kernel outside the initial namespace; AF_PACKET and raw sockets are already tested as denied.","kind":"task","title":"Address families that ignore the network namespace (AF_VSOCK) are not checked or blocked"}	blake3:B8PDAmKqEJyWMPLb5tU9gZg8t2_FYPE3GCx8hgx2Xek
1795187024661074	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24o	{"description":"Seen in the 2026-11-20 review. spec.md documents 'A path that does not exist is ignored', and src/sandbox.rs hide_path returns Ok for a missing path or a dangling symlink, with no message. A mistyped --hide therefore hides nothing and the run looks the same (verified: playpen run --no-network --hide /nonexistent-zz -- true exits 0 with the normal header). With Landlock this cannot open a socket (all host sockets are refused anyway), but --hide is also used for files, and under --without-landlock for sockets outside the hidden directories. Stricter choices: refuse to start (exit 2) or print a note in the header. Left for the owner because the current behaviour is documented and scripts may rely on hiding optional paths.","kind":"task","title":"A --hide path that does not exist is silently ignored"}	blake3:l6LtPEAS8UHcp9zT0pZoM82-4Xiv0TCusKuSQuOkns0
