1795198339390820	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2bo	{"description":"Priority 1 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A build owner can make unexpected outbound IP traffic fail CI even when the tool swallows the network error. Start here: it needs no new packet instrumentation.\n\nEvidence. A local run with two live listeners on sandbox loopback admitted TCP 9101 and denied TCP 9102. The child and wrapper exited 0; the final report counted one dropped packet. An unmapped getent lookup exited 2 with zero drops. This is an assertion about counted packets only.\n\nFirst slice. Add an explicit run assertion such as --fail-on-drop. Derive a structured pass / violated / unknown result from the final DropSummary, with the original child status and stop cause retained separately. Document nonzero wrapper status and its precedence over child success. Preserve the default exit contract. A nonzero child, revocation or abnormal termination never becomes success. Counter read failure under this assertion is indeterminate and nonzero.\n\nAcceptance. Run the two-port fixture with and without the option. Assert the child stays 0, only the opted-in wrapper fails, and the destination is in the report. Exercise clean traffic, child exit 2, refusal before launch, null counters, and a truncated destination list with nonzero total. No inference about DNS misses, Unix sockets, or unreachable packets. Use src/cli.rs::run, src/report.rs, and tests/run.rs.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","ci"],"size":"s","title":"Let CI fail when a successful command sends a denied packet"}	blake3:ZzqcKIBVTDsJrDk4fJRgdQyR0G8eyfkCjx0w2qj4Ja8
1795198340368514	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6ju	{"description":"Priority 2 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A human reviews the actual command, destinations, mapped names, expiration and host socket access. An agent launches that reviewed request or learns what it needs reviewed again.\n\nEvidence. rules --format json with one allow entry returned allowed, pass and 42 lines of nft text. rules takes neither --host nor --expose. run validates those separately in src/cli.rs::check / check_exposed. Pass bytes already have a digest. The preview pattern has a useful precedent in Terraform's speculative plan: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan .\n\nFirst slice. Add plan accepting the policy/command options of run, using one shared validation path and deterministic typed representation. Print a brief effective policy and optional versioned JSON including command, pass digest/labels, canonical destinations, hosts, requested hides/exposures, resolved display paths, socket guard and deadline. Identify settings resolved only at launch. For reviewed-scope execution add --expect-plan <digest>; rederive and compare before releasing the workload. Fingerprint the unambiguous canonical configuration and original argv bytes, not prose, clock-of-review, or report path. Use a versioned encoding. This is scope matching, not authority, proof of unchanged executable/file contents, or a guarantee the host can build a sandbox.\n\nAcceptance. A preview produces no child, rules, report side effects or network traffic. Reordering equivalent allow options is stable; changing an effective grant, host exposure, argv or selected pass bytes invalidates the pin. Reject a revoked/expired pass even if the old digest matches. Test repeated plans and a change between preview and launch; refine startup rechecks at the normal pre-exec boundary. Keep rules output compatible.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","approval"],"size":"m","title":"Preview the effective run and bind launch to the reviewed scope"}	blake3:EmeiBVrifOkJrgaIQ5ymj7DmshXX0ud_ObYnWWu1yPY
1795198341592854	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-yad	{"description":"Priority 3 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. An orchestrator can distinguish refusal, launch, revocation and completion while preserving child stdout as a data channel. No log scraping or repeated reads of a half-written report.\n\nEvidence. The final JSON file was zero bytes 0.4 s into a one-second job, then 557 bytes on exit. Both an invalid literal and a real getent child returned process status 2. Their reports say refused and ran respectively. Bubblewrap documents a dedicated JSON-lines status descriptor: https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap/blob/main/bwrap.xml .\n\nFirst slice. Add opt-in --events-fd N with one versioned JSON object per line on a caller-supplied, supervisor-owned writer above descriptors 0–2. Start with validated, starting, stopping and terminal events, stable reason codes, and child vs wrapper result. Name starting accurately: the existing Events::starting precedes exec. If exposing started, tie it to a confirmed boundary and document precisely what it proves. Emit a terminal record for post-parse policy refusals; document pre-parse/invalid-descriptor failures and abrupt EOF. No destinations, command arguments, or child text by default.\n\nAcceptance. Simultaneously capture child stdout, stderr and a status pipe for child exit 2, refusal 2 and pass revocation. Assert ordering and statuses, including partial lines and unknown future fields on the reader side. The fd is closed in every child/helper before user code. A closed or slow reader cannot delay expiry, heartbeat or cleanup; bound buffering and document how loss/incomplete streams are detected. No daemon or control commands. Start at src/sandbox.rs::Events, src/cli.rs and src/report.rs.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","agents"],"size":"m","title":"Give agent runners a private, typed lifecycle event stream"}	blake3:2Mu9ztznHC2UjV-saaiSYk9R-zWOcH8Gis0eyaQCyXc
1795198342436366	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3q2	{"description":"Priority 4 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. Someone launching an unattended job can require all applicable pass restrictions to be enforced. Today a name-limited destination can legally start with a human-readable warning that playpen only filters addresses; application ceilings also sit outside the firewall.\n\nFirst slice. Add structured enforcement coverage for the selected destinations and pass-wide ceilings: IP/port/protocol and time window enforced here, hostname allow-lists and application counters external. Provide opt-in --require-enforced to refuse before exec when applicable terms need another enforcer. Distinguish informational hostname metadata from a names restriction. Never infer enforcement from a hosts entry or a child process's name. Keep default behavior compatible and expose the gaps in the report, including on refusals.\n\nImplementation. Preserve typed coverage in src/pass.rs::FromPass instead of parsing the start-line note in src/cli.rs. PassWatch must notice newly applicable unsupported restrictions on re-read even if the IP/port allow rules are byte-for-byte identical. Use existing termination/grace semantics. Hallpass remains the owner of approval language and application counter semantics.\n\nAcceptance. A selected unrestricted label passes strict mode, a selected names restriction or operative global ceiling refuses, and an unselected label's name restriction alone does not refuse. An informational hostname is accepted. Change only a selected names restriction during a running strict job and verify it is stopped at recheck. Default warning and no-widening behavior still pass tests/pass.rs. No DNS proxy or application-protocol inspection.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","approval"],"size":"m","title":"Refuse a pass when the run requires guarantees playpen cannot enforce"}	blake3:L8aMCW_SbE6N3wplOXRZBHOFPrqJtE9xNbf3F7C4pA0
1795198342638474	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-8r5	{"description":"Priority 5 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. An offline build can proceed on a machine without pasta, and an agent scheduler can tell whether the requested mode was demonstrated on a host.\n\nEvidence. With PLAYPEN_PASTA pointing at an absent file, doctor exited 1 and skipped the namespace self-test; run --no-network -- true on the same host with the same override exited 0. src/doctor.rs::run currently gates all live probes on every basic prerequisite.\n\nFirst slice. Provide doctor --mode offline|egress with an explicit machine-readable capability result per requested mode. Factor shared prerequisites from mode-specific dependencies. Prove offline mode with a real isolated command and applicable local privilege/socket checks. Keep today's default full egress doctor and existing JSON consumers working. State unavailable, failed, and untested separately; report remedies only for the selected mode. Do not silently weaken the Unix-socket guard.\n\nAcceptance. An unavailable pasta binary fails egress and leaves genuinely working offline mode verified. A missing nft or unavailable user namespace cannot pass either mode. Simulate preflight failures and distinguish skipped from executed probes. Run the live offline case without a network interface beyond namespace loopback. Keep ordinary capability detection out of every run's startup hot path.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","onboarding"],"size":"s","title":"Make doctor test the mode an operator actually needs"}	blake3:1Kf-UR5R9ZNChL9lXz93Wa0OxtGIBdv1LlfRmfJupI0
1795198342861863	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-tbv	{"description":"Priority 6 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A developer can run a webhook consumer, package client, or API integration test against a known response without an account, an external service or a port on the host. A first-time user can try it with a stock HTTP client.\n\nSpike. An HTTPServer with one /items route and a standard-library HTTP client inside the same --no-network invocation returned 200 and {\"items\":[]}, with zero dropped packets. That verified the networking primitive. It did not validate a launcher, concurrent cleanup or an implementation of the proposed fixture protocol.\n\nFirst slice. Offer one opt-in fixture configuration for loopback-only runs, with exact method/path, status, bounded literal response body, one named URL environment variable and ephemeral port. A helper inside the sandbox binds first, then signals readiness before the payload starts. Keep both processes in the normal lifetime/privilege boundary, use an explicit file and never interpret it as a script. Ship a copyable exercise using a real client. No recording of real traffic, TLS interception, arbitrary sidecar supervision or host exposure.\n\nAcceptance. Fresh user runs the documented example and sees the exact body; an unmatched route gets a deterministic error, server and client share private loopback, and two concurrent runs get independent fixtures. Invalid fixtures or failed readiness run no client. Exit, signal, timeout and parent death leave no listener behind. Preserve --no-network's lack of external routes and existing PID-init/reaping guarantees. Start with a tiny end-to-end fixture alongside tests/run.rs before committing to a general format.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","testing"],"size":"m","title":"Run real clients against disposable HTTP fixtures with egress disabled"}	blake3:4219M9gfOSSin1HTEC1-3VyR540QNX3CzyzMHbyURpo
1795198343031826	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-39p	{"description":"Priority 7 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A team can commit examples of intended access next to an application and find accidental widening or narrowing before a job starts. Example: the artifact store's upload port must pass, its admin port must be denied.\n\nFirst slice. Add a small declarative test input of literal IP/port/protocol, expected allow/deny and optional case name. Read an explicitly selected run policy with current --pass/--only or --allow semantics. Restrict these assertions to outbound application tuples; state how private loopback and special kernel traffic are classified or excluded. Output each failing case, the expected decision and which grant or exclusion decided it. Return nonzero on mismatches and refuse invalid policies distinctly. No probes or DNS and no grant-creation syntax.\n\nImplementation. Share the effective policy from the plan bone and model matching from typed AllowRule, Ports and the same excluded_addrs logic used by ruleset::render. Hallpass permits already checks pass authorization; this feature specifically tests the firewall decision after run-specific narrowing, with multiple assertions and CI status. Keep the oracle honest by comparing against actual filter behavior on sandbox-local listeners in integration tests.\n\nAcceptance. Cover IPv6, wrong protocol, overlapping CIDRs, network/broadcast exclusions, explicit singletons, /31 endpoints, selected labels and a revoked pass. Show a deliberately failing case caught both statically and by the kernel fixture. Running the policy test itself never sends a packet or loads nft. Leave general-purpose policy languages out of v1.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","testing"],"size":"m","title":"Let teams test expected allow and deny decisions as code"}	blake3:gWfTpXlNwRhrZM6HudqGx55FFdyWJEiuKJEg0EVj4t8
1795198343319462	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-34f	{"description":"Priority 8 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. After a successful run an owner can ask which granted destinations had any observed use, and which deserve review for a narrower future pass. A failing connection can still count as an admitted packet.\n\nEvidence. src/ruleset.rs renders per-destination accept rules without counters and checks established/related traffic first. The mixed-port run reported the denied port but had no allowed-use fields. nftables supports opt-in rule counters and their placement affects what they count: https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Counters .\n\nFirst slice. Count packets matching each explicit grant's accept rule, with bounded-size telemetry, a deterministic rule ID and provenance for pass label/port range. Reuse the end-of-run table readback. Document first-match behavior for overlapping grants and explicitly distinguish no observed match, shadowed/ambiguous, and unavailable. Established traffic accepted earlier means these are neither connection counts nor total bytes exchanged. Do not recommend removing a grant after one quiet run.\n\nAcceptance. Use a live local fixture with two grants and one denial. Confirm activity only on the matching grant, no inflation of drop totals, and no change to admissions. Exercise duplicate/overlapping grants, missing counters, plain --allow rules and a no-network report. Retain rule-load verification and existing report consumers. Start opt-in if counter readback costs materially affect large passes.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","observability"],"size":"m","title":"Show which granted destinations actually admitted traffic"}	blake3:62kHDWyb4Ysc5Sbj7-mGb930LCQjvh9GWmb6G63eZfw
1795198343501668	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3t9	{"description":"Priority 9 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A maintainer can attach a diagnostic summary to an issue or CI artifact without disclosing command arguments, owner names, paths or destination literals. They can still show whether the sandbox ran, why it stopped, and whether the counters were available.\n\nEvidence. A harmless marker passed as an argument to echo appeared verbatim in Report.command. Existing fields can also carry host mappings, paths, pass identity, free-text errors and per-destination addresses.\n\nFirst slice. Make a separate reduced report selectable at collection time; an agent can write it directly without first leaving a full report on disk. Use a positive field allowlist of booleans, bounded totals, public enums and numeric status. Preserve outcome, child status, guard mode, aggregate drops, completeness and revocation/expiry class. Exclude argv including executable path, raw messages, digests, labels, endpoint details, environment, and arbitrary future fields by construction. Document that child output, caller-created files and timing/aggregate information have their own disclosure properties.\n\nAcceptance. Put distinct sentinel strings in command, mapping, pass owner/label/path, exposed path, and a refusal message. Verify none appear in reduced success/refusal/error artifacts; zero and unavailable counters remain distinguishable. Full report compatibility stays intact. Tests must fail if a new free-text field is accidentally copied into the reduced view. Implement in src/report.rs and CLI report selection, with no heuristic secret matching.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","privacy"],"size":"s","title":"Export a shareable run summary without copying private arguments"}	blake3:qdlPZ-eBKJfK3sZgBhF98pV1LgDaeon8hZ7-DZ6nYbY
1795198343753543	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-239	{"description":"Priority 10 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. During a dependency upgrade a reviewer can answer “which blocked destinations appeared for the first time?” without diffing retry counts, raw argv or JSON formatting. Works with full reports from current releases.\n\nFirst slice. Add an offline report comparison keyed on canonical IP, protocol and port. Surface newly observed denied destinations, no-longer-observed ones, and separately whether effective run settings are comparable (grants, network mode, name mappings, socket exposures and guard). Counts are context, not default change triggers. Provide a compact CI predicate for new denials, plus text/JSON. If policy changed, call that out before interpreting observations. Add positive sightings only once grant-usage telemetry exists; it is not a prerequisite.\n\nAcceptance. Reordering and changing retry counts produce no new endpoint. A new denied tuple is highlighted; a replaced grant is a policy change. Missing counters, unstarted jobs, truncated lists, reduced reports or later incompatible schemas cannot yield a clean equality claim. Compare only known evidence; never infer that no-longer-observed means allowed or fixed. Parse report files as inert data, with input bounds; no command execution or auto-updating a baseline. Exercise tests with both historical and new report shapes.","kind":"task","labels":["ux-roadmap","review"],"size":"s","title":"Highlight changed network behavior between two runs"}	blake3:UsIy3gqk3wSkDUddjW8YDmVsHxIBZPaC21N-O4hMP2g
1795198360547264	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-39p	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-6ju"}	blake3:FGvsqbg61Dai2CXLs_gct49QXM_NbPoQ_HxC2lmjqQU
1795198360981318	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-6ju	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-3q2"}	blake3:kWqaZDwmuWVvC8O6nFCfJAFTkHXQVaOsqqOaXvnDK28
1795198361083055	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-239	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-34f"}	blake3:Bbyr-CQvLBAfuvJlWAPzo1b3jFzV9UFwL37Vj3LVmRY
1795198361187939	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-3t9	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-yad"}	blake3:msHXmAxMH2hBrk2G0iGqW9ArbkSLFFe0G3nN_t_pchw
1795198361295119	codex	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-22v	{"body":"Discovery at fa1b9b1. Compared 100 distinct candidates across ten areas, each scored 1–5 on user impact, breadth across humans and agents, implementation feasibility, and evidence. Weighted those 3:2:2:1; broke ties by scope and distinct user decision. Top ten in build-priority order: 1. bn-2bo, 2. bn-6ju, 3. bn-yad, 4. bn-3q2, 5. bn-8r5, 6. bn-tbv, 7. bn-39p, 8. bn-34f, 9. bn-3t9, 10. bn-239. Search label ux-roadmap. Scores ranged from 40 to 35 of 40 in the selected set. Each selected item records an independently testable first slice. Top recommendation is the CI verdict. Only the executable policy examples require the preview's shared effective-policy representation; the other first slices can start independently.\n\nObserved on this host with the locked release binary: one successful local TCP connection and one refused port produced child/wrapper 0, dropped.packets 1; getent hosts fixture.invalid in --no-network produced ran/2/zero whereas a hostname in --allow produced refused/2/null. The report was 0 bytes during sleep and contained the complete JSON after exit. A synthetic argv marker appeared in the full report. An unavailable PLAYPEN_PASTA made doctor exit 1 and skip its self-test while --no-network -- true exited 0. A temporary local HTTP fixture returned status 200 with {\"items\":[]} in the same private loopback-only run. No proposed features were implemented by these spikes. Research references: upstream bubblewrap JSON status FDs, Terraform plan, and nftables counters; URLs are in the relevant bones."}	blake3:Kq7w7mFPbNm4BNV8f1cSmPcCfl8ij_jdc0wd9tXHUAk
1795198361401002	codex	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-22v	{"reason":"Researched and ranked 100 candidates, ran local workflow spikes, and created ten open, independently scoped implementation bones with acceptance criteria.","state":"done"}	blake3:T7SKFUaZgmNy73da4V2a60zSTg3Lpv885zb-m5zErtM
1795199027507755	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-hw7	{"description":"At b9a0455, rendered_rules_have_a_fixed_shape builds arbitrary strings and discards parse failures before rendering. With PROPTEST_CASES=4096 and PROPTEST_RNG_SEED=20261120 the test passed, but all 14,331 candidates failed parsing. The empty-policy behavior has value and already has a simple deterministic witness.\n\nGenerate valid AllowRule values by construction. Exercise both families, both protocols, single ports and ranges, multiple grants, and overlapping networks. Keep malformed-input parser tests separate. Check the rendered grants against independently computed expected fields and ordering constraints. Make coverage of the named classes deterministic, so a change to the generator cannot silently remove a class. A temporary mutation that omits a generated grant must turn the test red and shrink to a small witness. Run through the existing cargo test entry point and record the seed used. Strategy reference: https://proptest-rs.github.io/proptest/proptest/tutorial/higher-order.html","kind":"bug","labels":["correctness","ruleset"],"size":"m","title":"Make the firewall property test exercise valid grants on every nonempty case"}	blake3:lmTotGPGKutcbqDuH_oU0BbA8D3mAxVHAlnWVv2B1Ak
1795199027843858	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-k0v	{"description":"For a fixed, valid pass and evaluation time, every packet tuple admitted by a selected subset of labels must also be admissible under the full pass's packet-level scope. At b9a0455 pass::load selects destinations before conversion, while approval::excluded_addrs depends on the collection of rules. The pinned Hallpass decision implementation is an independent reference for the full-pass address exclusions. This interaction deserves a direct check.\n\nBuild an in-memory decision oracle from the full pass and compare it with the actual selection and conversion path. Enumerate every subset of small generated passes with overlapping and disjoint scopes, duplicate semantics, both families, boundary addresses, and intersecting port/protocol grants. Include permitted and denied witnesses. Document fixed sandbox exceptions and which application-layer fields fall outside this comparison. Use a stricter synthetic full-pass fixture to check that the inclusion assertion can fail. Preserve any discovered disagreement as a regression and resolve it against the documented contract.","kind":"task","labels":["correctness","approval"],"size":"l","title":"Verify that selecting fewer Hallpass destinations can only narrow packet permissions"}	blake3:PqRSIQp5J04GyX3qELP5k0nT2phedrGDsTV9JMuxUTA
1795199028724443	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13h	{"description":"At b9a0455 a synthetic nft fixture supplied a total of 2 dropped packets and one destination with 3 packets. ruleset::parse_counters returned Ok with destinations_truncated=false. This reproduces a reporting error on inconsistent input. Whether a live nft dump can produce the same snapshot requires separate evidence.\n\nDefine which observations justify a complete breakdown, keeping a known total usable if only detail is uncertain. Check malformed or missing entries, duplicate identities, per-destination capacity truncation, checked arithmetic, and totals on either side of the detail sum. Trace results through the public JSON report and preserve its documented compatibility. Add positive controls for an exact zero and an exact nonempty snapshot and regression fixtures for contradictions and overflow. Test in debug and release. Record the actual snapshot guarantees of supported nft versions before promising exactness under concurrent traffic.","kind":"bug","labels":["correctness","reporting"],"size":"m","title":"Make drop reports distinguish complete, partial, and contradictory counts"}	blake3:uYKc8TlI-sZQzpNOuU8XY79ER98v9Fxe_5Ow9hTIvC8
1795199028954102	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1c6	{"description":"At b9a0455 ruleset::verify_loaded receives JSON and an allow-rule count. The existing empty-load regression tests a missing installation. A rule count and chain policies cannot establish that the installed addresses, ports, protocols, verdicts, and accounting behavior match the approved policy.\n\nPass expected policy semantics into verification before the command may start. Normalize the small nft expression vocabulary playpen emits, including table family, chain hooks and priorities, rule order, fixed exceptions, and final drop accounting. Account for equivalent syntax found in supported nft versions and ignore volatile handles and counter values. Reject an unsupported expression whose meaning is material. Accept readback of real disposable playpen installations on a supported Linux host. Reject a purely in-memory fixture with the same rule count but a narrower port interval. State the supported readback forms and the trust placed in nft and the kernel. Reference: https://www.netfilter.org/projects/nftables/manpage.html","kind":"task","labels":["correctness","ruleset"],"size":"l","title":"Compare the installed nft rules with the policy playpen intended to load"}	blake3:SB6JMsigPL0hjkYtW_O6Le2vE1ucGQ-IowjibHlWSZ0
1795199029420022	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2bp	{"description":"A Kani 0.68.0 spike used the production mask_host_bits body from b9a0455. For arbitrary IPv4 bits, valid prefix, and bit position, the output retained precisely the bits within the prefix. Three reachability covers passed. Clearing one extra bit in a temporary mutant failed the assertion.\n\nBind checked-in proof harnesses to the production functions. Extend the independent bit specification to IPv6, network containment/overlap and the documented IPv4 edge convention, and verify inclusive Ports::contains boundaries. Specify constructors' valid domains, exercise both decision outcomes and extreme prefixes, and check every reported unwind bound. Include a reproducible command and a representative red mutation. If a solver budget limits a property, report the exact bound and unsolved cases. These arithmetic claims apply to the pure primitives. Kernel behavior has separate acceptance evidence. Reference: https://model-checking.github.io/kani/tutorial-loop-unwinding.html","kind":"task","labels":["correctness","verification"],"size":"m","title":"Prove the production address and port primitives at their boundaries with Kani"}	blake3:KOlBm5Q5_48ZC88-NOJMKpoDo_70SVoGpgSY8dF-l4w
1795199029747517	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1th	{"description":"Deadline::passed combines wall and monotonic time, PassWatch::judge processes pass changes, and the parent and namespace init implement rechecks, termination grace, and heartbeat loss. Existing tests cover individual decisions and selected live timelines. Verify their composition against the revocation promise in docs/DESIGN.md.\n\nDrive production decisions with explicit clock readings and bounded event sequences. Cover exact expiry, wall-clock movement in either direction, delayed polls, edits or revocation around startup, and a delayed parent. Verify that the original deadline stays binding, a terminal decision cannot revert, and repeated input cannot restart termination grace. Include witnesses for continued execution of a valid pass, clean natural exit, and enforcement. A temporary strict-versus-inclusive boundary mutation must fail. Publish the event bound and scheduling assumptions. Use native Linux checks for the clock and signal behavior the model assumes, and distinguish a decision at a deadline from when a process can actually be scheduled to act. Reference: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/clock_gettime.2.html","kind":"task","labels":["correctness","revocation"],"size":"l","title":"Check expiry and revocation decisions across clock changes and supervisor delays"}	blake3:r9jtFZOQvqTy2hbV9WnZEuJZevlEhFraqMRLhoL2S3M
1795199030012212	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2xw	{"description":"A caller may safely retry some commands only when playpen can establish that the first attempt never executed. In src/sandbox.rs the parent sends GO, init watches a close-on-exec pipe, and ParentState parses status lines. Specify what observations justify definite startup refusal, confirmed execution, or an unknown outcome, then carry that distinction through the public exit report.\n\nExercise the production state transitions against an independent table of permissible outcomes. Enumerate bounded, per-writer ordered traces with partial reads, buffered complete lines, interruption, EOF and child exit at different stages. A transport failure after GO alone must not justify a definite never-started report. Check non-vacuity with a clean execution, an authoritative exec refusal, and an ambiguous loss of status. Make an overconfident classification fail a synthetic trace, and verify normal execution and missing-binary behavior through the real CLI using harmless commands. Record the trace bounds and the ordering guarantees assumed.","kind":"task","labels":["correctness","supervision"],"size":"l","title":"Verify when the supervisor may tell a caller that a command never started"}	blake3:CCoF7sHAUZCdehXdguAvub9JE0y9onG-WlKgUtRwTJI
1795199030450576	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6z3	{"description":"Several host-dependent tests in tests/run.rs, pass.rs, sockets.rs, gateway.rs and terminal.rs print SKIPPED and return successfully when a prerequisite is missing. A passing cargo test result therefore cannot tell a release reviewer which kernel behaviors were exercised. Existing bones track specific unqualified environments and assumptions. This task makes the evidence observable across suites.\n\nProvide an opt-in qualification entry point that lists scenarios, prerequisites, and an executed/unsupported/failed result for each. Let a qualification job declare which scenarios it requires and exit unsuccessfully if any of those could not run. Keep ordinary developer tests usable on constrained machines. Demonstrate with one supported native scenario that really exercises the sandbox and one deliberately unavailable prerequisite that leaves the scenario visibly unsupported and fails a required run. Preserve failure status from assertions. Document how to read this evidence alongside portable unit tests and pure Rust proofs.","kind":"task","labels":["correctness","testing"],"size":"m","title":"Make Linux qualification report which sandbox scenarios actually ran"}	blake3:a4zTdYYowX6PjcJFYy64JXkKWxICp63SoTWJYEpLgWQ
1795199036294192	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-39p	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-k0v"}	blake3:fSKPefKYo8XnbH1b8g675HYqnSWM6G4zXRvJYrd_OsQ
1795199036389975	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2bo	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-13h"}	blake3:zpew-bzE6cs8n9uHltvZiGw7o8t-50ZXmohQNrAcCXE
1795199036485941	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-yad	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-2xw"}	blake3:uTpCqXqz3QtZWQ31zgXP64TpooWHmxTDu69jLDZORiQ
1795199036594005	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-30l	{"link_type":"related_to","target":"bn-6z3"}	blake3:hOJgPVlEqjcYKng5JXB6ejHLO8TcQOoAlFbkYADof44
1795199349645553	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-no8	{"description":"Manual exit gate. Review the typed grant generator, arithmetic proof results and their bounds, and subset inclusion results. On supported Linux, review a valid installed readback and a harmless narrower fixture rejected before launch. Confirm the current spec explains what is checked at startup. Record the host and any unverified behavior before signing off.","kind":"goal","labels":["goal:manual"],"size":"l","title":"Qualify approved packet scope from Hallpass selection to installed rules"}	blake3:gGdKDn6HheYER594veuGZ6GzJnIl0GDZtjjoc50P7uY
1795199349820020	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bg	{"description":"Manual exit gate. Trace a clean run, a refusal, loss of status, revocation and expiry through the production CLI or a clearly identified model when delivery cannot be guaranteed. Reconcile the report and lifecycle stream with child evidence and the documented timing assumptions. Review Linux signal evidence and the README revocation promise before closing.","kind":"goal","labels":["goal:manual"],"size":"l","title":"Qualify command startup, revocation, and terminal reporting"}	blake3:hYCeZZe9cIt6hl1BuhYi_kiwXayAotL67WxsJq1gjpE
