1795199410375435	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2c0	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-2bo"}	blake3:Q7IZ76Ut8DC0bqma_vsdsdaGByURLIqUvDEKmAD4YMY
1795199410454823	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2bo	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 1 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A build owner can make unexpected outbound IP traffic fail CI even when the tool swallows the network error. Start here: it needs no new packet instrumentation.\n\nEvidence. A local run with two live listeners on sandbox loopback admitted TCP 9101 and denied TCP 9102. The child and wrapper exited 0; the final report counted one dropped packet. An unmapped getent lookup exited 2 with zero drops. This is an assertion about counted packets only.\n\nFirst slice. Add an explicit run assertion such as --fail-on-drop. Derive a structured pass / violated / unknown result from the final DropSummary, with the original child status and stop cause retained separately. Document nonzero wrapper status and its precedence over child success. Preserve the default exit contract. A nonzero child, revocation or abnormal termination never becomes success. Counter read failure under this assertion is indeterminate and nonzero.\n\nAcceptance. Run the two-port fixture with and without the option. Assert the child stays 0, only the opted-in wrapper fails, and the destination is in the report. Exercise clean traffic, child exit 2, refusal before launch, null counters, and a truncated destination list with nonzero total. No inference about DNS misses, Unix sockets, or unreachable packets. Use src/cli.rs::run, src/report.rs, and tests/run.rs.\n\nDocument child versus wrapper exit precedence in docs/spec.md and include a short README CI example. A known positive total determines violation even when the breakdown is partial."}	blake3:kiQsf2AtFw7ZrRgIk34bZle25Wa1311mJAPLISCIwt8
1795199410535316	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-yad	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 3 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. An orchestrator can distinguish refusal, launch, revocation and completion while preserving child stdout as a data channel. No log scraping or repeated reads of a half-written report.\n\nEvidence. The final JSON file was zero bytes 0.4 s into a one-second job, then 557 bytes on exit. Both an invalid literal and a real getent child returned process status 2. Their reports say refused and ran respectively. Bubblewrap documents a dedicated JSON-lines status descriptor: https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap/blob/main/bwrap.xml .\n\nFirst slice. Add opt-in --events-fd N with one versioned JSON object per line on a caller-supplied, supervisor-owned writer above descriptors 0–2. Start with validated, starting, stopping and terminal events, stable reason codes, and child vs wrapper result. Name starting accurately: the existing Events::starting precedes exec. If exposing started, tie it to a confirmed boundary and document precisely what it proves. Emit a terminal record for post-parse policy refusals; document pre-parse/invalid-descriptor failures and abrupt EOF. No destinations, command arguments, or child text by default.\n\nAcceptance. Simultaneously capture child stdout, stderr and a status pipe for child exit 2, refusal 2 and pass revocation. Assert ordering and statuses, including partial lines and unknown future fields on the reader side. The fd is closed in every child/helper before user code. A closed or slow reader cannot delay expiry, heartbeat or cleanup; bound buffering and document how loss/incomplete streams are detected. No daemon or control commands. Start at src/sandbox.rs::Events, src/cli.rs and src/report.rs.\n\nPublish the event schema, loss semantics and a reader example in docs/spec.md. Demonstrate a slow reader concurrently with normal supervision; only promise startup certainty that the authoritative supervisor evidence can support."}	blake3:yEGzhTQPFu3qCcbmRUe3jHawT-hE5HkXZNcz-DgPOIs
1795199410613492	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-39p	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 7 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A team can commit examples of intended access next to an application and find accidental widening or narrowing before a job starts. Example: the artifact store's upload port must pass, its admin port must be denied.\n\nFirst slice. Add a small declarative test input of literal IP/port/protocol, expected allow/deny and optional case name. Read an explicitly selected run policy with current --pass/--only or --allow semantics. Restrict these assertions to outbound application tuples; state how private loopback and special kernel traffic are classified or excluded. Output each failing case, the expected decision and which grant or exclusion decided it. Return nonzero on mismatches and refuse invalid policies distinctly. No probes or DNS and no grant-creation syntax.\n\nImplementation. Share the effective policy from the plan bone and model matching from typed AllowRule, Ports and the same excluded_addrs logic used by ruleset::render. Hallpass permits already checks pass authorization; this feature specifically tests the firewall decision after run-specific narrowing, with multiple assertions and CI status. Keep the oracle honest by comparing against actual filter behavior on sandbox-local listeners in integration tests.\n\nAcceptance. Cover IPv6, wrong protocol, overlapping CIDRs, network/broadcast exclusions, explicit singletons, /31 endpoints, selected labels and a revoked pass. Show a deliberately failing case caught both statically and by the kernel fixture. Running the policy test itself never sends a packet or loads nft. Leave general-purpose policy languages out of v1.\n\nDocument one passing and one deliberately failing case in docs/spec.md, and verify the example exit statuses with the CLI."}	blake3:JXPyug46nUj6IgKLwnC1P2kC5SVoZtRgOPSCCTL3eNA
1795199410694871	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3q2	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 4 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. Someone launching an unattended job can require all applicable pass restrictions to be enforced. Today a name-limited destination can legally start with a human-readable warning that playpen only filters addresses; application ceilings also sit outside the firewall.\n\nFirst slice. Add structured enforcement coverage for the selected destinations and pass-wide ceilings: IP/port/protocol and time window enforced here, hostname allow-lists and application counters external. Provide opt-in --require-enforced to refuse before exec when applicable terms need another enforcer. Distinguish informational hostname metadata from a names restriction. Never infer enforcement from a hosts entry or a child process's name. Keep default behavior compatible and expose the gaps in the report, including on refusals.\n\nImplementation. Preserve typed coverage in src/pass.rs::FromPass instead of parsing the start-line note in src/cli.rs. PassWatch must notice newly applicable unsupported restrictions on re-read even if the IP/port allow rules are byte-for-byte identical. Use existing termination/grace semantics. Hallpass remains the owner of approval language and application counter semantics.\n\nAcceptance. A selected unrestricted label passes strict mode, a selected names restriction or operative global ceiling refuses, and an unselected label's name restriction alone does not refuse. An informational hostname is accepted. Change only a selected names restriction during a running strict job and verify it is stopped at recheck. Default warning and no-widening behavior still pass tests/pass.rs. No DNS proxy or application-protocol inspection.\n\nDocument the strict-mode refusal and recheck behavior in docs/spec.md and distinguish application restrictions in the README pass example."}	blake3:J0_fyhCD-dQKboqIMG1pTG6IJ6qzr0qNXtZozlFgMYo
1795199410777533	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3t9	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 9 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A maintainer can attach a diagnostic summary to an issue or CI artifact without disclosing command arguments, owner names, paths or destination literals. They can still show whether the sandbox ran, why it stopped, and whether the counters were available.\n\nEvidence. A harmless marker passed as an argument to echo appeared verbatim in Report.command. Existing fields can also carry host mappings, paths, pass identity, free-text errors and per-destination addresses.\n\nFirst slice. Make a separate reduced report selectable at collection time; an agent can write it directly without first leaving a full report on disk. Use a positive field allowlist of booleans, bounded totals, public enums and numeric status. Preserve outcome, child status, guard mode, aggregate drops, completeness and revocation/expiry class. Exclude argv including executable path, raw messages, digests, labels, endpoint details, environment, and arbitrary future fields by construction. Document that child output, caller-created files and timing/aggregate information have their own disclosure properties.\n\nAcceptance. Put distinct sentinel strings in command, mapping, pass owner/label/path, exposed path, and a refusal message. Verify none appear in reduced success/refusal/error artifacts; zero and unavailable counters remain distinguishable. Full report compatibility stays intact. Tests must fail if a new free-text field is accidentally copied into the reduced view. Implement in src/report.rs and CLI report selection, with no heuristic secret matching.\n\nDocument the shareable report's field allowlist and compatibility story in docs/spec.md, with a sanitized example."}	blake3:1qFisbpj23_28XKEYWlKWVuY7hszTYP0JnQR0zcW29k
1795199410861375	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-239	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 10 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. During a dependency upgrade a reviewer can answer “which blocked destinations appeared for the first time?” without diffing retry counts, raw argv or JSON formatting. Works with full reports from current releases.\n\nFirst slice. Add an offline report comparison keyed on canonical IP, protocol and port. Surface newly observed denied destinations, no-longer-observed ones, and separately whether effective run settings are comparable (grants, network mode, name mappings, socket exposures and guard). Counts are context, not default change triggers. Provide a compact CI predicate for new denials, plus text/JSON. If policy changed, call that out before interpreting observations. Add positive sightings only once grant-usage telemetry exists; it is not a prerequisite.\n\nAcceptance. Reordering and changing retry counts produce no new endpoint. A new denied tuple is highlighted; a replaced grant is a policy change. Missing counters, unstarted jobs, truncated lists, reduced reports or later incompatible schemas cannot yield a clean equality claim. Compare only known evidence; never infer that no-longer-observed means allowed or fixed. Parse report files as inert data, with input bounds; no command execution or auto-updating a baseline. Exercise tests with both historical and new report shapes.\n\nDocument the diff exit contract and the outcome when reports lack comparable evidence. Use fixtures from the current pre-feature report schema."}	blake3:IhMvbKqUwy60UJLGwNPNr64f_iSf-t039Va1R0UFmG8
1795199410940075	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-34f	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 8 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. After a successful run an owner can ask which granted destinations had any observed use, and which deserve review for a narrower future pass. A failing connection can still count as an admitted packet.\n\nEvidence. src/ruleset.rs renders per-destination accept rules without counters and checks established/related traffic first. The mixed-port run reported the denied port but had no allowed-use fields. nftables supports opt-in rule counters and their placement affects what they count: https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Counters .\n\nFirst slice. Count packets matching each explicit grant's accept rule, with bounded-size telemetry, a deterministic rule ID and provenance for pass label/port range. Reuse the end-of-run table readback. Document first-match behavior for overlapping grants and explicitly distinguish no observed match, shadowed/ambiguous, and unavailable. Established traffic accepted earlier means these are neither connection counts nor total bytes exchanged. Do not recommend removing a grant after one quiet run.\n\nAcceptance. Use a live local fixture with two grants and one denial. Confirm activity only on the matching grant, no inflation of drop totals, and no change to admissions. Exercise duplicate/overlapping grants, missing counters, plain --allow rules and a no-network report. Retain rule-load verification and existing report consumers. Start opt-in if counter readback costs materially affect large passes.\n\nDocument first-match accounting and missing evidence in docs/spec.md. Include the optional instrumentation in expected ruleset verification whenever enabled."}	blake3:uYtIfRVEKwSD2dKY7A3WuN9PcMpdS_6bdFw561yKZLw
1795199411024282	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-tbv	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 6 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A developer can run a webhook consumer, package client, or API integration test against a known response without an account, an external service or a port on the host. A first-time user can try it with a stock HTTP client.\n\nSpike. An HTTPServer with one /items route and a standard-library HTTP client inside the same --no-network invocation returned 200 and {\"items\":[]}, with zero dropped packets. That verified the networking primitive. It did not validate a launcher, concurrent cleanup or an implementation of the proposed fixture protocol.\n\nFirst slice. Offer one opt-in fixture configuration for loopback-only runs, with exact method/path, status, bounded literal response body, one named URL environment variable and ephemeral port. A helper inside the sandbox binds first, then signals readiness before the payload starts. Keep both processes in the normal lifetime/privilege boundary, use an explicit file and never interpret it as a script. Ship a copyable exercise using a real client. No recording of real traffic, TLS interception, arbitrary sidecar supervision or host exposure.\n\nAcceptance. Fresh user runs the documented example and sees the exact body; an unmatched route gets a deterministic error, server and client share private loopback, and two concurrent runs get independent fixtures. Invalid fixtures or failed readiness run no client. Exit, signal, timeout and parent death leave no listener behind. Preserve --no-network's lack of external routes and existing PID-init/reaping guarantees. Start with a tiny end-to-end fixture alongside tests/run.rs before committing to a general format.\n\nAdd the tested offline recipe to README and document fixture limits and process lifetime in docs/spec.md."}	blake3:xFNE_FKxq9lXyDoIUsCvU0zdiGyRa7gU-WrRrMQj6dk
1795199411109947	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-8r5	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 5 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. An offline build can proceed on a machine without pasta, and an agent scheduler can tell whether the requested mode was demonstrated on a host.\n\nEvidence. With PLAYPEN_PASTA pointing at an absent file, doctor exited 1 and skipped the namespace self-test; run --no-network -- true on the same host with the same override exited 0. src/doctor.rs::run currently gates all live probes on every basic prerequisite.\n\nFirst slice. Provide doctor --mode offline|egress with an explicit machine-readable capability result per requested mode. Factor shared prerequisites from mode-specific dependencies. Prove offline mode with a real isolated command and applicable local privilege/socket checks. Keep today's default full egress doctor and existing JSON consumers working. State unavailable, failed, and untested separately; report remedies only for the selected mode. Do not silently weaken the Unix-socket guard.\n\nAcceptance. An unavailable pasta binary fails egress and leaves genuinely working offline mode verified. A missing nft or unavailable user namespace cannot pass either mode. Simulate preflight failures and distinguish skipped from executed probes. Run the live offline case without a network interface beyond namespace loopback. Keep ordinary capability detection out of every run's startup hot path.\n\nDocument one offline and one egress doctor invocation, their JSON capability results, and prerequisites in README and docs/spec.md."}	blake3:n-n0Id1-Xt6cHXB9-gQ6rU86CnfhLfbHE4AdSgDykqk
1795199411191490	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-13h	{"field":"description","value":"At b9a0455 a synthetic nft fixture supplied a total of 2 dropped packets and one destination with 3 packets. ruleset::parse_counters returned Ok with destinations_truncated=false. This reproduces a reporting error on inconsistent input. Whether a live nft dump can produce the same snapshot requires separate evidence.\n\nDefine which observations justify a complete breakdown, keeping a known total usable if only detail is uncertain. Check malformed or missing entries, duplicate identities, per-destination capacity truncation, checked arithmetic, and totals on either side of the detail sum. Trace results through the public JSON report and preserve its documented compatibility. Add positive controls for an exact zero and an exact nonempty snapshot and regression fixtures for contradictions and overflow. Test in debug and release. Record the actual snapshot guarantees of supported nft versions before promising exactness under concurrent traffic.\n\nDocument what a known total, incomplete breakdown and unavailable snapshot mean for existing JSON consumers in docs/spec.md."}	blake3:K8TEoisM8yhJwrPIlNm3VIWyizd6iETW3I6pga7s3mg
