1795394825813111	lead	itc:AQ		item.assign	bn-1w3	{"action":"assign","agent":"lead"}	blake3:oqPYvgUqRBfqgJM0KekWFHaaBLuVm_TWdo0_4Z-4Kns
1795395706531971	lead	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-1w3	{"body":"A synchronized three-process probe also ruled out inspecting /proc pending bits as a repair. After a genuine group send, the workload consumed SIGUSR1 while two supervisors waited; after two PID-only sends the workload had received nothing. At the supervisor decision point the thread and shared pending masks were identical in both histories for all three processes: (0, 512), (0, 512), (0, 0). Forwarding needs opposite decisions. Linux kernel/signal.c places both ordinary kill destinations on the shared pending queue. Local witness: target/bn-1w3-lead/pending_scope.py."}	blake3:Pe7ip8lBGPPRf71Tg5QLS4_8x1AKAQsYiN7DJoAXdwc
1795397354238900	enforced-topology	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-vcn	{"description":"Scourge bn-qpi: on Linux 7.2.8-arch1-2, Playpen 32677f2dde4449d578f9a5bf3417f442c252c7a4 (binary sha256 4bf7ade4fa677e15319b825d2bdd2c79ee247586adda1abd40f3b9880654548b), Teapot 6238d5a78a6514466d54ad16dd5737f43c1d4961 and Hallpass 44f749ac158e8378705bab80d855ed0a1f634a54: bind disposable HTTP fixture to host 172.16.0.35:PORT; pass permits exactly that TCP address/port; start Teapot with --enforce and send GET. Teapot reports enforcement on, but approved request gets connect-failed / connection refused. Outside pass port is refused by Teapot. Host Playpen doctor reports egress available. Playpen src/pasta.rs OPTIONS disables host loopback mapping and namespace port forwarding; this does not by itself establish the cause of this failure. Alternative: run fixture and Teapot together under playpen run --no-network with loopback pass and Teapot --enforce; nested Playpen doctor fails with EPERM remount inside sandbox; Teapot source also declares loopback pass non-enforceable. Please identify a supported enforced local fixture topology or track a safe capability to provide one. Reproduction and evidence in Scourge workspace bn-qpi scripts/enforced-topology.py and fixtures/contracts/enforced-{nonloopback,offline}.jsonl; hashes and pass ledger in captures. This is a local feature/compatibility blocker, not proof of bypass.","kind":"bug","title":"Enforced sandbox cannot reach approved fixture on host non-loopback address"}	blake3:hA0uMiJN-vAPaMvpWSA4CVFNKaZBsXZGI9Ba7OpaMMM
1795403686302743	claude	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-1w3	{"body":"Fixed in b8f760f (main). Attached default mode now delivers one copy for a group send. Mechanism: the supervisor forks a witness (W) that stays in its process group and reports each kill(2) signal it receives; init leaves that group once the command is forked. A signal the supervisor receives is passed on when W did not receive it (sent to the supervisor alone) or when both W and init received it (playpen's processes signalled one by one, as killall/pkill/pidof do); it is not passed on when W received it and init did not (group send), unless the command has left the group (a job-control shell), which init checks. Init out of the group also stops kill 0 inside the sandbox being forwarded a second time (was 3 receipts per send). No terminal or foreground-group change, so pipeline siblings, /dev/tty and fg/bg are untouched. Known residual, by design: a signal sent by PID to the supervisor and W but not to init is taken for a group send and not passed on; it needs a hand-made PID list. If W cannot be had (fork fails, killed, no answer in 1 s) the supervisor forwards everything as before. Verified: acceptance test attached_and_new_session_pid_and_group_signals_have_one_delivery passes (4 paced sends, 4 receipts, PID and group, attached and --new-session); new tests attached_signal_for_each_playpen_process_has_one_delivery (ascending and descending PID order), attached_group_signal_reaches_a_shell_that_left_the_group, attached_group_signal_from_inside_has_one_delivery; each fails when its part of the routing is removed. Manual PTY run: group sends to a stopped job, then fg, gave one receipt per delivered signal. Full cargo test (70 unit, 33 terminal, 53 run, all suites) and all 12 qualification scenarios pass; clippy clean. DESIGN.md (Attached jobs), spec sections 5 and 8, README updated."}	blake3:qT_mJfzgZWGIl3wADCj8Af0FGWChM-UpblZlRT36O10
1795403686315580	claude	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-vcn	{"body":"Resolved in b8f760f (main). Cause, reproduced on this host with a listener on 172.16.0.35: pasta --config-net copies the addresses of the host's default-route interface into the sandbox, so 172.16.0.35 is the sandbox's own address there. The connection is delivered inside the sandbox, never reaches pasta or the host, and is refused; the filter accepted it, so no drop is counted. --map-host-loopback none is not involved. Supported enforced local topology (verified, no privileges): have the fixture listen on a host address the sandbox does not get. On a real host that is an address on a second interface. Without one, build a throwaway host: unshare --user --map-root-user --net; a dummy interface with an address and the default route (pasta copies this one); a second dummy interface with the fixture address (e.g. 198.51.100.7/32); start the fixture and then Teapot/playpen side by side in that namespace as the caller's uid (unshare --user --map-user=UID --map-group=GID). The pass approves the fixture address and port. Nothing leaves the machine. Test: tests/gateway.rs a_host_service_is_reached_at_an_address_the_sandbox_does_not_have (reached at the second address, refused at the copied one). Recipe in README 'Reach a test service on this machine' and DESIGN.md 'The host's own addresses'. Playpen change: at start it now prints 'NOTE: ADDR is approved, but the sandbox has the same address on its own interface ...' when a rule approves such an address on its own, so this failure explains itself. Nested playpen is not a route: Landlock on the outer run refuses the inner one's mounts (cannot make mounts private: EPERM; the inner run starts only if the outer uses --without-landlock); recorded in spec section 8. Not built: reaching the host's own copied address itself (would need pasta --map-guest-addr plus address translation in the ruleset); open a feature bone if Scourge needs that instead of the topology above."}	blake3:pgJ_pOJOBYrYL-m6X1EZXhJoQpNPZzGXpciVrVhuq-0
1795403686331040	claude	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-1w3	{"reason":"fixed and merged in b8f760f","state":"done"}	blake3:vtTBNtOEu6IE4OggboPAjw0tfPRzQ9KjPJSfFxiNObw
1795403686336817	claude	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-vcn	{"reason":"fixed and merged in b8f760f","state":"done"}	blake3:-ez5TVQSK4GB635Nz744Njk-qE1NlntOYadKn891T5I
1795403686347556	claude	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-3bg	{"body":"bn-1w3 is done (b8f760f): default attached mode now delivers a group signal once; evidence in bn-1w3 and DESIGN.md 'Attached jobs'. This gate was held open for default-mode signal handling; left open for the manual review it calls for."}	blake3:7_IFQg9DhImdcOVsRccGj3yzh7R012wBoyhKpVDRigg
1795404233850540	claude	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-3bg	{"body":"Closing review at b8f760f. All six children are done. Signal evidence: default attached mode now gives one receipt per group or PID signal (bn-1w3; four terminal tests, each failing when its part of the routing is removed), so the reason this gate stayed open is gone. README revocation promise re-checked against the changed supervisor loop: with 12 processes inside flooding SIGUSR1 at the process group, and separately at PID 1, the deadline notice came 0.02 s and 0.00 s after the deadline, the run ended after the 3 s grace, and Ctrl-Z still stopped a detached ticker (0 ticks while stopped). Full cargo test on main: every suite passed except one run of terminal where private_tty_inner_job_control_keeps_the_outer_output_pipeline_running timed out while the machine was at load average 36 from other projects' test runs (suite took 11 s, usually 6 s); it then passed 6 of 6 alone and the terminal suite passed 8 of 8. Not reproduced, cause not established; private-tty mode does not use the witness. All 12 qualification scenarios executed ok before the merge."}	blake3:YQsASdrW1b9JCSS-MjFX2tB9Veids8PyZFRga4jkNks
1795404233896507	claude	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-3bg	{"reason":"children done; signal evidence and README revocation promise reviewed at b8f760f","state":"done"}	blake3:9a7xsAPw2iPLe879TAX3fYjYv_RYAF0bz594Dc78YAM
1795405388650976	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-jku	{"description":"Grouping goal for the ten ideas kept from a round of one hundred (2026-11-22). Each child is independent unless it says otherwise. Suggested order is the 'Priority N of 10' line in each child. Shared rule for every child: a new option enters playpen plan and the plan digest, the start line and the report, and the published guarantees in README.md, docs/spec.md and docs/DESIGN.md change in the same merge as the code. Close this goal when the children are done or deliberately dropped.","kind":"goal","labels":["ideas-2026-11"],"title":"Ten next capabilities: in-kernel approval end, live denials, names, evidence and reach (idea round, November 2026)"}	blake3:LigpZv6doyAYW-5xHiqFpF-ky1ClufSnmGefQP6ajzU
1795405563113167	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2kk	{"description":"Priority 3 of 10. Needs \"End egress in the firewall itself when the approval ends or is revoked\" for the cut set, and \"Report dropped destinations while the command is still running\" for the stop variant.\n\nUser outcome. An operator who must not leave scope can say `--on-drop cut`: the first packet the firewall refuses also closes every approved destination, in the kernel, with no polling delay. With `--on-drop stop` playpen also ends the command, as it does when a pass is revoked. Today a tool that strays keeps working against the approved targets until it exits, and the warning arrives at the end.\n\nEvidence. Checked on 2026-11-22 in a throwaway namespace (nft 1.1.7): with `meta mark @cut drop` early in the chain and `add @cut { meta mark }` on the refusal path, a ping to the approved address succeeded, one ping to an unapproved address was refused, and the next ping to the approved address was refused too. Later packets stop at the cut rule, so the drop sets hold only what was sent up to the trip and the report can name the destination that tripped it.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--on-drop cut`: add the self-tripping statement before the drop counter. The command keeps running and sees its connections fail. Report `egress_cut` with the cause, and print which destination tripped it. Packets refused after the trip go to the cut counter, not to the unapproved-destination total.\n- `--on-drop stop`: as cut, and when the supervisor learns of the trip from the live readback it stops the command with the existing SIGTERM, grace, SIGKILL sequence. Report `stopped_by_drop: true` with a `stop_reason`. A stop by drop never exits 0.\n- The option is in `playpen plan`, the digest, the start line and the shareable report.\n\nAcceptance.\n- Fixture with two approved listeners and one unapproved address: requests to the first succeed, one packet goes to the unapproved address, then requests to both approved listeners fail. The listeners' logs agree and the report names the unapproved destination.\n- stop: the command ends within the documented readback interval plus grace, with the specified exit status and report fields.\n- Without the option the ruleset and the report are what they are today.\n- Document what commonly trips it without being an attack: multicast name lookups (mDNS, LLMNR), a client trying a DNS server, a cloud SDK probing the metadata address, a second address family. That is why it is opt-in.\n\nOut of scope. Tripping on Unix-socket refusals or on name lookups.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","ruleset","approval"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"s","title":"Tripwire: close all egress at the first packet to an unapproved destination"}	blake3:Bt4BQTBdLf36uaSR2qhmob1gClLxC1MKiIe9r-NUlog
1795405564161907	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-arr	{"description":"Priority 1 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. When the approval's end time arrives, no packet leaves the sandbox from that moment, whether the supervisor is running, stopped or slow. When playpen detects a revoked or withdrawn pass, egress closes at detection and stays closed through the 3 second grace before SIGKILL. The README can then remove \"traffic to approved destinations during the shutdown window\" for expiry and narrow it to the detection delay for revocation.\n\nEvidence. Today only the supervisor enforces the end time and the pass re-read. README \"How a pass-backed run ends\" says the command can still reach approved destinations during detection and shutdown. DESIGN.md \"SIGSTOP to playpen itself\" says the end time is not enforced for 5 seconds while the supervisor is stopped. Two ruleset facts were checked on 2026-11-22 in a throwaway namespace on the Arch host (nft 1.1.7). (1) `meta time >= EPOCH counter name \"late\" drop` passed a packet before the time and dropped the next one after it. (2) With `meta mark @cut drop` and a one-element dynamic set of type mark, all egress closed as soon as `nft add element ... cut { 0 }` ran, and reopened after a flush. The command cannot set a packet mark: it has no capability in the namespace that owns the network.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Ruleset: when the approval has an end time, add a rule that refuses every non-loopback packet at or after it. It must come before the established/related accept, so that accept moves below the loopback accepts. Do the same in the input chain. Add the `cut` set and its rule at the same place. Each has its own named counter.\n- Supervisor: on a pass stop or a deadline, tell the sandbox side to add the `cut` element before SIGTERM is sent. One new byte on the existing down pipe.\n- The start-up readback learns both rules, so a run does not start unless they are installed as rendered.\n- Report: packets refused after the end get their own field and line (\"N packet(s) refused after the approval ended\"). They are not counted as unapproved destinations and do not change `--fail-on-drop`.\n\nAcceptance.\n- `--until` a few seconds ahead, supervisor sent SIGSTOP before the end: a looping sender inside reaches a local fixture before the end and not once after it, and the listener's own log agrees.\n- A pass revoked mid-run: the fixture receives nothing after the \"stopping\" notice while the command is still alive in its grace period.\n- Loopback inside the sandbox keeps working after the end. A run without an end time loads no time rule. `playpen rules` prints the new rules. Existing revocation, expiry and qualification scenarios pass.\n- Find out how `nft -j` prints the time back (the text listing shows local wall time) and normalise it in the readback comparison.\n\nOpen decision. Whether a command may still finish politely over the network during the grace (a clean FIN or a protocol goodbye). Recommendation: no, the approval has ended. Record the choice in DESIGN.md, and note there that `meta time` follows the wall clock, as the supervisor's check does.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","revocation","ruleset","correctness"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"End egress in the firewall itself when the approval ends or is revoked"}	blake3:5LlaIBYQ3q4FMxALAIgRHwnABynh9UXdPqjeKGPHF8I
1795405564162151	claude	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2kk	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-arr"}	blake3:rpRcZuvhwvZVciwpIYaTeYpf464pH8vJUlVhIhW6hws
1795405565277375	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1cy	{"description":"Priority 2 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. During a long run (a scan, an agent session, a server) the operator or the agent runner learns that a packet was refused when it happens, not at exit: one `denied` record on `--events-fd` per new destination, and a stderr line on request. A runner can then stop, ask for approval or change course while the session is alive.\n\nEvidence. Drop counts are read once, at teardown, and the lifecycle stream (src/cli/event_pipe.rs) has no denial record. The ruleset already keeps the named counter `dropped` and the per-destination sets, and the sandbox side already sends `drops` to the supervisor on the up pipe. The data and the channel exist; only the timing is missing.\n\nFirst slice.\n- The sandbox-side process that reads the counters at teardown also reads the `dropped` counter on a fixed interval (2 s, the pass re-read cadence) and lists the sets only when the counter has moved. It sends new destinations up the existing pipe.\n- The supervisor emits `{\"event\":\"denied\",\"details\":{\"address\",\"protocol\",\"port\",\"packets\"}}` once per new destination. With `--live-drops` it also prints `playpen: dropped: 198.51.100.7 tcp port 80 (not approved)`. Live lines are capped: the first 8 destinations, then one \"more in the final report\" line.\n- The final report still comes from the teardown readback and does not change.\n\nAcceptance.\n- A command that sends to a denied address and then sleeps: the event (and the line, when asked for) appears within one interval plus scheduling slack, while the command is alive. More packets to the same destination produce no second record.\n- A burst to more than 8 destinations stays bounded on stderr and complete in the report.\n- A run with no drops has unchanged output. Without `--live-drops`, attached and `--private-tty` terminals see no new lines.\n- Measure the cost of the interval read with a full 1024-entry set and state it in DESIGN.md. If starting nft every interval is too heavy, read the counter over netlink.\n\nOpen decision. Whether stderr lines should be on by default when stderr is not a terminal. Recommendation: events always, stderr opt-in for the first release.\n\nLater. An NFLOG group read inside the namespace would make this immediate instead of interval-based.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","observability","agents"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"Report dropped destinations while the command is still running"}	blake3:glOJ7lHB52DezbA_AVVI4f5ElbGZLdxiCLZNC9iBLKI
1795405565328931	claude	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2kk	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1cy"}	blake3:X2I1glCeg5vUW-DUPOTNR9Sb0p3zyPJi2FEl4FwuZK8
1795405566151043	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bcd	{"description":"Priority 4 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. The most common first failure under playpen is \"could not resolve host\", and today nothing records it. A lookup inside the sandbox goes to loopback, is refused there, and is not a dropped packet, so the report is silent (spec: the drop assertion \"counts dropped IP packets, not DNS misses\"). After this change the report lists the names the command asked for and marks the ones with no `--host` mapping, with the advice to add one.\n\nEvidence. src/resolver.rs generates a resolv.conf with no nameserver; resolvers then try 127.0.0.1 port 53, where nothing listens. The ruleset accepts loopback. src/fixture.rs already runs a small listener inside the sandbox's network.\n\nFirst slice.\n- A stub on sandbox loopback, UDP and TCP port 53, bound before the command starts, while the sandbox side can still bind a low port. Write `nameserver 127.0.0.1` into the generated resolv.conf.\n- It reads the question only. For a `--host` name it answers with the mapped A or AAAA records. For every other name it answers that the name does not exist. It forwards nothing, anywhere.\n- It keeps up to 256 distinct names with a count each and sends them up at teardown. Report field `lookups: {\"names\":[{\"name\",\"count\",\"mapped\"}],\"truncated\"}` and a text block: \"The command looked up names that have no --host mapping: ...\".\n- The names come from the workload. Accept only letters, digits, hyphen, underscore and dots, at most 253 bytes; escape everything else before it reaches a terminal or the report.\n\nAcceptance.\n- `getent hosts unmapped.example` fails as fast as it does today, and the report lists the name once with its count.\n- A client that does not read /etc/hosts (`dig`, Node's `dns.resolve`) resolves a `--host` name through the stub.\n- A lookup containing terminal escape sequences, or an over-long label, never reaches the terminal raw.\n- The command cannot bind port 53 and cannot signal or replace the stub.\n- Works with `--no-network`. Not in the shareable report. `playpen compare` treats lookups as observations, like dropped tuples.\n\nOut of scope. Resolving real names. DNS over HTTPS or TLS, which already appears as dropped packets to the resolver's address.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","observability","onboarding"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"Record the names the command tried to look up"}	blake3:LWIIqdrzseR-UPl5d0RRMDtFX5HsNfP0V1CBp0Rgo6s
1795405567075516	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ja	{"description":"Priority 5 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. For a quick run, `--allow-name api.example.com:443/tcp` replaces finding the address by hand and typing it twice (`--allow` and `--host`). playpen looks the name up once, on the host, before anything starts. It approves exactly the addresses returned, maps the name to them inside the sandbox and prints them on the start line. There is still no DNS inside, so the command can only use the pinned addresses. With a pass, `--pass-hosts` maps each selected destination's `hostname` to that destination's address, with no lookup at all.\n\nEvidence. README: \"The pass's host names are never resolved for you; map them with --host\", and its example repeats 192.0.2.10 in the pass and on the command line. The Hallpass format has an optional `hostname` per destination (PASS-FORMAT.md: informational, grants nothing). `--host NAME=ADDR` already requires ADDR to be approved.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--allow-name NAME:PORTS/PROTO`, repeatable, refused together with `--pass`. The supervisor does the lookup with the host's resolver before any namespace exists: A and AAAA, at most 16 addresses per name (more is a refusal that lists them). A failed lookup is a refusal: exit 2, nothing run.\n- `playpen plan` performs the same lookup and shows the addresses. The digest covers them, so `--expect-plan` refuses a run whose lookup result differs from the reviewed one.\n- Start line and report show the name, the addresses and \"looked up at start\". Report field `resolved`.\n- `--pass-hosts`: for each selected pass destination with a `hostname` and a single address, add the hosts entry. A destination that is a network is skipped with a NOTE. No lookup and no new grant.\n\nAcceptance.\n- Tests use a resolver fixture that needs no outside network and cover one address, both families, a failed lookup and too many addresses.\n- A plan made when the name had one address refuses to run when it has another.\n- `--allow-name` with `--pass` is refused. `--pass-hosts` adds no rule: `playpen rules` output is the same with and without it.\n- README says plainly: the lookup leaves this host before the sandbox exists; addresses are not refreshed during a run; a name on shared hosting approves whatever else lives at those addresses.\n\nOpen decision. Whether the digest should bind the name only, so that a reviewed plan survives an address change. Recommendation: bind the addresses, because those are what the reviewer approved.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","approval","onboarding"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"Approve a name: look it up once at start and pin the addresses"}	blake3:h6dVfuQ1N9ZomtNX7ppcbljywtUKRlBvW25mAQRsOYY
1795405567743213	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1cn	{"description":"Priority 6 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. After a run the operator can see each address, port and protocol the command opened a flow to inside the approved scope, and `playpen suggest REPORT` prints the smallest set of `--allow` and `--host` options that would have admitted the same traffic. A wide first grant (a /24, a port range) becomes a reviewed, narrow one from evidence, and an engagement report can list what was touched.\n\nEvidence. `--report-grant-use` counts packets per grant rule, not which hosts inside a network grant were contacted (spec, \"Grant use in private reports\"). The drop path already records address, protocol and port in bounded dynamic sets. Checked on 2026-11-22 in a throwaway namespace (nft 1.1.7): `... add @seen4 { ip daddr . meta l4proto . th dport } accept` on a grant rule recorded three distinct tuples with correct packet counts. It counts only the first packet of each flow, because later packets match the established rule first.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Opt-in `--report-contacts`: sets `seen4` and `seen6` (1024 entries each, the shape of the drop sets), filled on every explicit grant's accept rule, included in the start-up readback check, read at teardown with the drops.\n- Report `contacted: {\"destinations\":[{\"address\",\"protocol\",\"port\",\"packets\"}],\"truncated\"}`. State that `packets` counts flow starts, not volume.\n- `playpen suggest REPORT [--format json]` reads a full report as inert data and prints exact `--allow` lines for the contacted tuples, plus the `--host` entries that point at them. It refuses when the record is truncated or unavailable or the run did not start. It never prints anything wider than the original grants.\n- Works together with `--report-grant-use`. Not allowed with `--shareable-report`.\n\nAcceptance.\n- Fixture with a /24 grant over a port range and three contacted tuples: the report lists exactly those. The `suggest` output, given back to `playpen run`, admits the same requests and drops a fourth.\n- Overflow past 1024 sets `truncated`, and `suggest` refuses.\n- Without the option the ruleset is unchanged.\n- The text output repeats the existing caution: one quiet run does not show that a later run needs less.\n\nOut of scope. A mode that blocks nothing. A deliberately wide `--allow` with this option already serves as a learning run, and it stays an explicit approval.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","observability","review"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"Record each destination the command contacted and suggest the narrowest allow list"}	blake3:hOiohTkYyqj9O9uvrR7Nywvs6O81sXEkjnot8fG5JEk
1795405568817912	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2h7	{"description":"Priority 7 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. An operator can give the owner a record of exactly what was sent. `--capture FILE` writes a pcap of every packet that crossed the sandbox's outside interface, and the report says when the run started and ended, so the capture, the report and the pass window line up.\n\nEvidence. pasta has `-p, --pcap FILE` (\"Log tap-facing traffic to pcap file\"); src/pasta.rs does not pass it. Refused packets are rejected in the output hook before they reach that interface, so the capture should hold approved traffic and its replies only. The test below has to confirm that. The report has no time fields (src/report.rs).\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--capture FILE`: refused with `--no-network`. Create the file safely before pasta starts (new file, mode 0600, no symlink following, never overwrite), and decide whether pasta gets the path or an inherited descriptor.\n- Report: `started_at` and `ended_at` (RFC 3339 UTC; start is when the command is released, end is when it is reaped) in every report of a run that started. `capture: {\"file\",\"bytes\",\"sha256\"}`, computed after pasta has exited.\n- The start line names the capture file. The shareable report never gets the capture fields; whether it gets the two times is an allowlist decision under spec \"Shareable reports\".\n\nAcceptance.\n- Run against a local fixture with one approved and one denied destination: the pcap parses, contains the approved exchange and no packet to the denied address, and its digest matches the report.\n- For `sleep 2`, `ended_at - started_at` matches the run time within a second.\n- An existing file, a symlink and an unwritable directory are refusals before anything runs.\n- A run stopped by its deadline still leaves a complete capture.\n- Docs: the capture holds payloads, including credentials sent in clear. Treat it like the pass. `playpen compare` ignores the new fields.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","reporting","evidence"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"s","title":"Keep a packet capture of what left the sandbox, and timestamp the run"}	blake3:gl5lzx0jfOUuqNgE8Dn3OL8xBQxktZDlAMeTWYShpVY
1795405569989897	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-gnj	{"description":"Priority 8 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. playpen can guard programs that listen: a development server, a tool with a web UI, an agent's callback endpoint. `--publish 8080` lets a browser or a test on this machine connect to port 8080 inside the sandbox while the command's outbound traffic stays limited to the approvals. Today nothing outside can connect in, so such programs cannot be used under playpen.\n\nEvidence. src/pasta.rs starts pasta with `--tcp-ports none`, and the input chain accepts only loopback and established traffic. Checked on 2026-11-22 on the Arch host with pasta alone: `-t 127.0.0.1/18999` delivered a connection from host loopback to a listener inside. That test showed two things that shape the work. The connection arrives on the pasta interface, so a server bound only to 127.0.0.1 inside did not receive it; it has to bind the wildcard address. And the source address seen inside is this host's address. This has not been tried with playpen's ruleset loaded.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--publish [HOST_ADDR:]PORT[/tcp]`, repeatable, TCP only. The host side binds 127.0.0.1 unless another address is given; a wildcard host address has to be typed out.\n- pasta gets the forward. The input chain gets one accept per published port on the outside interface. Replies leave as established traffic; no outbound rule is added.\n- Start line and report (`published`) list each forward, like `--expose`. The option is in the plan and the digest.\n- A port already in use on the host is a refusal before anything runs. Define `--publish` with `--no-network` (recommendation: allowed, meaning pasta runs with no outbound grant).\n\nAcceptance.\n- A listener inside is reached from the host on the published port and not on another port. The command still cannot open a connection to an unapproved address, and the attempt is counted.\n- The command cannot use the forward to reach the host: a connection from inside to the host's address and the published port stays inside the sandbox (DESIGN.md, \"The host's own addresses\").\n- UDP and unpublished ports are refused as before. Qualification gains a scenario.\n- README says: anything that can reach the host-side port (any local user, a web page through the browser) can talk to the command, and the command can answer it. It also says a server must listen on the wildcard address.\n\nOut of scope. UDP, port ranges, mapping to a different port inside.","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","network"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"Publish a port so this machine can reach a server inside the sandbox"}	blake3:yLcgeR8pEKM_n6WN270LKlz-sVh_eevOEz_vYD6hqmM
1795405570892072	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-i89	{"description":"Priority 9 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. The first entry under \"playpen does not guard against\" goes away: non-IP transports such as VM sockets (AF_VSOCK). On a cloud VM, under WSL2 or in a development VM, a sandboxed command can no longer open a socket to the hypervisor or host that bypasses the nft table and the drop counts. The command can create Unix, IPv4, IPv6 and netlink sockets and no others.\n\nEvidence. README threat model, and spec section 8: \"host-specific non-IP transports, including AF_VSOCK ... A missing VSOCK sysctl establishes no general isolation guarantee\". playpen installs no seccomp filter today. The command already runs with no_new_privs, which is what an unprivileged filter needs.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Before exec, install a seccomp filter: `socket` and `socketpair` with a family other than AF_UNIX, AF_INET, AF_INET6 or AF_NETLINK fail with EAFNOSUPPORT.\n- Close the two known ways around a syscall filter. io_uring can create a socket without the socket call, so `io_uring_setup` fails with ENOSYS unless `--allow-io-uring` is given (shown on the start line and in the report). On x86_64 the 32-bit and x32 entry points get the same family check or are refused; `socketcall` cannot be inspected and is refused.\n- Supported on x86_64 and aarch64. On another architecture playpen refuses to run without `--without-socket-filter`.\n- Report `socket_families` (`unix_ip_netlink` or `unfiltered`). doctor gains a probe. The plan and the digest carry the setting.\n- First find out whether Landlock on the supported kernels can restrict socket creation by family. If it can, use it where present, because it would cover io_uring too.\n\nAcceptance.\n- A probe inside tries AF_VSOCK, AF_PACKET, AF_BLUETOOTH, AF_ALG, AF_XDP and a family number above the known range: each fails with EAFNOSUPPORT. TCP, UDP, Unix sockets and `getaddrinfo` still work; curl, ssh, python and a Go binary run unchanged.\n- An io_uring socket attempt fails by default. A 32-bit test binary cannot create an AF_VSOCK socket.\n- The filter is still in force in children and after exec.\n- Decide and document AF_ALG (kernel crypto, local only) before release.\n- README and spec section 8 are rewritten: what is now refused, and what remains (the daemon behind an exposed Unix socket, kernel bugs).","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","correctness","isolation"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"m","title":"Refuse socket families that the packet filter cannot see"}	blake3:APEAitBLGPk3pQ9sbWW_wbEEsrSt97voMilPLQSUDFg
1795405571750689	claude	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-265	{"description":"Priority 10 of 10.\n\nUser outcome. A reviewer signs off one file, and the operator or the CI job runs that file: `playpen plan --out run.plan.json ...`, then `playpen run --plan run.plan.json`. Today the second step has to repeat every option exactly and carry the digest by hand (`--expect-plan`). That is easy to get wrong and awkward to keep in a repository or a ticket.\n\nEvidence. `playpen plan --format json` already returns the complete validated run and its digest: `plan` holds command, cwd, allowed, hosts, until, expose, hide, display, network, pass, socket guard and fail_on_drop under schema `playpen.plan.v1`. Nothing reads it back.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `plan --out FILE` writes that JSON (new file, mode 0600).\n- `run --plan FILE` takes the command and every policy setting from the file. Policy options or a command on the command line are refused beside it. Reporting options (`--report`, `--format`, `--events-fd`) remain. `--expect-plan` may still be given and must match.\n- Before anything starts: the file is read as inert data with a size limit and the file checks a pass gets (not a symlink, not writable by others). The digest is recomputed from the `plan` object and must equal the stored one. The plan then goes through the same validation as a typed command, so an edited file cannot express anything the CLI could not. A pass named in the plan is re-read and its bytes must match the recorded digest.\n- The report records the plan file's digest.\n\nAcceptance.\n- Round trip: a plan file produces the same start line, ruleset and report as the typed command.\n- Any edit to the file (a wider grant, another command, another cwd) is refused with exit 2 and \"nothing was run\".\n- A plan with a pass refuses when the pass has changed, expired or been revoked.\n- A plan with an unknown schema version is refused with a message that names the version.\n- An `until` in the past refuses as it does today.\n- Docs: the plan binds arguments and settings, not the bytes of the executable or its inputs (the existing caveat).","kind":"task","labels":["ideas-2026-11","approval","review"],"parent":"bn-jku","size":"s","title":"Review a plan file, then run exactly that file"}	blake3:D74gTPdnA_AuIToLchyDel1fhYPEqFgp3OVC9z_GPPE
1795405821533072	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2ja	{"field":"title","value":"Map a pass's own hostnames to their addresses with --pass-hosts"}	blake3:6Gb2HlEYUtQXbx5zkCgjYZc-9CUApB4VoY59zfG7Pbw
1795405821537695	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2ja	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 7 of 8.\n\nUser outcome. With a pass, `--pass-hosts` gives the command a local name for each selected destination that the owner named, so the operator does not copy addresses out of the pass onto the command line. playpen does no lookup and adds no grant: the name and the address both come from the pass the owner wrote.\n\nEvidence. README: \"The pass's host names are never resolved for you; map them with --host\", and its example repeats 192.0.2.10 in the pass and in `--host app.example.test=192.0.2.10`. The Hallpass format has an optional `hostname` per destination (PASS-FORMAT.md: informational, grants nothing, never an IP address). `--host NAME=ADDR` already requires ADDR to be approved.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--pass-hosts`, valid only with `--pass`. For each selected destination (after `--only`) that has a `hostname` and a single address, add the hosts entry NAME -> address. Two destinations with the same hostname give that name both addresses.\n- A destination that is a network, or has no hostname, is skipped; a skipped network with a hostname gets a NOTE.\n- An explicit `--host` for the same name wins, and the start line says so.\n- The start line and the report's `hosts` show the generated entries, marked as coming from the pass. The option is in `playpen plan` and the digest.\n\nAcceptance.\n- A pass with two named destinations: `getent hosts NAME` inside returns the pass's address for each, and curl by name reaches a local fixture.\n- `playpen rules` output is identical with and without the option.\n- `--only LABEL` limits the generated entries to the selected destinations.\n- `--pass-hosts` without `--pass` is a usage error.\n- A pass edit that changes a hostname mid-run is handled by the existing rule for pass changes; say which in the spec.\n- README replaces the \"map them with --host\" sentence and keeps the statement that playpen resolves nothing.\n\nDropped from this bone (2026-11-22). `--allow-name`, which would have looked a name up on the host at start and approved the addresses returned. Reason: playpen exists so that a name resolving to an unexpected address cannot widen the scope; with that option playpen itself would approve whatever DNS answered. The earlier text is in this bone's history."}	blake3:ME_4ZUYsKq9tySFzj0T0QcjoRKPRYDQO-JU43q4lMO4
1795405821538436	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2ja	{"field":"size","value":"s"}	blake3:xg9SEdAFcXolq88wIM2dUdkp1HjVRA0UBTcUGC-LtuQ
1795405821549678	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-i89	{"field":"title","value":"Find out whether the kernel can keep non-IP sockets (AF_VSOCK) out of the sandbox"}	blake3:J3nfYO24sTg6VicKSDrWFukyn9kC7pYVTQRLjhK8KcI
1795405821555733	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-i89	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 8 of 8. Investigation; changes no behaviour.\n\nOutcome. A decision, backed by tests, on whether and how playpen should keep the command from using socket families the nft table cannot see, AF_VSOCK first. The README line \"non-IP transports provided by the host or hypervisor\" becomes exact: which families, on which kernels, reach what.\n\nEvidence. README threat model, and spec section 8: \"host-specific non-IP transports, including AF_VSOCK ... A missing VSOCK sysctl establishes no general isolation guarantee\", with a link to the kernel's VSOCK namespace mode. playpen installs no syscall filter today.\n\nWork.\n- Family sweep: a probe that tries `socket()` for every address family number inside a default playpen sandbox and records the errno, on the Arch host and in the Arch guest from docs/qualification-second-linux.md. For each family that can be created, state what it can reach from a fresh network namespace with no capabilities.\n- VSOCK: in a guest with a vsock device, try to connect from inside the sandbox to the host CID, under each value of the kernel's VSOCK namespace mode setting that the supported kernels offer. Record which values isolate the sandbox and whether playpen can check the setting without privilege.\n- Landlock: find out whether the Landlock ABI playpen requires (9), or a newer one, can restrict socket creation by family, and test it if so.\n- Write the results as a table in DESIGN.md and correct README and spec section 8 where they are vaguer or stronger than the evidence.\n\nAcceptance.\n- The table exists, with kernel versions and the commands that produced each row.\n- One recommendation, with reasons, among: nothing more is needed; `playpen doctor` and the start line report when VSOCK is reachable; a Landlock rule; a seccomp family filter.\n- A follow-up bone exists for the recommended change, or the decision that none is needed is recorded here.\n\nOut of scope. Installing a filter. An earlier version of this bone specified a seccomp filter (families other than Unix, IPv4, IPv6 and netlink refused; io_uring refused by default; 32-bit entry points handled). It was set aside on 2026-11-22 as too much new mechanism for a gap that only exists on machines with a vsock device. That text is in this bone's history and is the starting point if the recommendation is a filter."}	blake3:VRza-rL4cogvTHL0MEQjMU0QCBpOO9roViuoNt1DlEg
1795405821557584	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-i89	{"field":"size","value":"s"}	blake3:xPB_M5h23n8i8laA3BGUj8KfW2hZgN1idGbxHR2ya98
1795405821568566	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-gnj	{"field":"urgency","value":"punt"}	blake3:11akJ540EB1DO3Z4v5MK701VMwUHmTWjz8f2vwr9JC4
1795405821589667	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-gnj	{"field":"description","value":"Parked on 2026-11-22 (urgency punt). Reason: this is the only idea in the round that opens a path instead of closing or reporting one, and no current use needs it. Take it up when there is a concrete case, for example a proxy that runs inside playpen while its clients run outside.\n\nUser outcome. playpen can guard programs that listen: a development server, a tool with a web UI, an agent's callback endpoint. `--publish 8080` lets a browser or a test on this machine connect to port 8080 inside the sandbox while the command's outbound traffic stays limited to the approvals. Today nothing outside can connect in, so such programs cannot be used under playpen.\n\nEvidence. src/pasta.rs starts pasta with `--tcp-ports none`, and the input chain accepts only loopback and established traffic. Checked on 2026-11-22 on the Arch host with pasta alone: `-t 127.0.0.1/18999` delivered a connection from host loopback to a listener inside. That test showed two things that shape the work. The connection arrives on the pasta interface, so a server bound only to 127.0.0.1 inside did not receive it; it has to bind the wildcard address. And the source address seen inside is this host's address. This has not been tried with playpen's ruleset loaded.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--publish [HOST_ADDR:]PORT[/tcp]`, repeatable, TCP only. The host side binds 127.0.0.1 unless another address is given; a wildcard host address has to be typed out.\n- pasta gets the forward. The input chain gets one accept per published port on the outside interface. Replies leave as established traffic; no outbound rule is added.\n- Start line and report (`published`) list each forward, like `--expose`. The option is in the plan and the digest.\n- A port already in use on the host is a refusal before anything runs. Define `--publish` with `--no-network` (recommendation: allowed, meaning pasta runs with no outbound grant).\n\nAcceptance.\n- A listener inside is reached from the host on the published port and not on another port. The command still cannot open a connection to an unapproved address, and the attempt is counted.\n- The command cannot use the forward to reach the host: a connection from inside to the host's address and the published port stays inside the sandbox (DESIGN.md, \"The host's own addresses\").\n- UDP and unpublished ports are refused as before. Qualification gains a scenario.\n- README says: anything that can reach the host-side port (any local user, a web page through the browser) can talk to the command, and the command can answer it. It also says a server must listen on the wildcard address.\n\nOut of scope. UDP, port ranges, mapping to a different port inside."}	blake3:D0opycPIVa50T-B_YkE-NwUgV_LFatSaDFEZbtSuLs4
1795405821610876	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-arr	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 1 of 8.\n\nUser outcome. When the approval's end time arrives, no packet leaves the sandbox from that moment, whether the supervisor is running, stopped or slow. When playpen detects a revoked or withdrawn pass, egress closes at detection and stays closed through the 3 second grace before SIGKILL. The README can then remove \"traffic to approved destinations during the shutdown window\" for expiry and narrow it to the detection delay for revocation.\n\nEvidence. Today only the supervisor enforces the end time and the pass re-read. README \"How a pass-backed run ends\" says the command can still reach approved destinations during detection and shutdown. DESIGN.md \"SIGSTOP to playpen itself\" says the end time is not enforced for 5 seconds while the supervisor is stopped. Two ruleset facts were checked on 2026-11-22 in a throwaway namespace on the Arch host (nft 1.1.7). (1) `meta time >= EPOCH counter name \"late\" drop` passed a packet before the time and dropped the next one after it. (2) With `meta mark @cut drop` and a one-element dynamic set of type mark, all egress closed as soon as `nft add element ... cut { 0 }` ran, and reopened after a flush. The command cannot set a packet mark: it has no capability in the namespace that owns the network.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Ruleset: when the approval has an end time, add a rule that refuses every non-loopback packet at or after it. It must come before the established/related accept, so that accept moves below the loopback accepts. Do the same in the input chain. Add the `cut` set and its rule at the same place. Each has its own named counter.\n- Supervisor: on a pass stop or a deadline, tell the sandbox side to add the `cut` element before SIGTERM is sent. One new byte on the existing down pipe.\n- The start-up readback learns both rules, so a run does not start unless they are installed as rendered.\n- Report: packets refused after the end get their own field and line (\"N packet(s) refused after the approval ended\"). They are not counted as unapproved destinations and do not change `--fail-on-drop`.\n\nAcceptance.\n- `--until` a few seconds ahead, supervisor sent SIGSTOP before the end: a looping sender inside reaches a local fixture before the end and not once after it, and the listener's own log agrees.\n- A pass revoked mid-run: the fixture receives nothing after the \"stopping\" notice while the command is still alive in its grace period.\n- Loopback inside the sandbox keeps working after the end. A run without an end time loads no time rule. `playpen rules` prints the new rules. Existing revocation, expiry and qualification scenarios pass.\n- Find out how `nft -j` prints the time back (the text listing shows local wall time) and normalise it in the readback comparison.\n\nOpen decision. Whether a command may still finish politely over the network during the grace (a clean FIN or a protocol goodbye). Recommendation: no, the approval has ended. Record the choice in DESIGN.md, and note there that `meta time` follows the wall clock, as the supervisor's check does."}	blake3:DuZSghiJ7qoZyFXb9e2REInvfDb2HoYJVRhgQA9RhPQ
1795405821633114	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1cy	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 2 of 8.\n\nUser outcome. During a long run (a scan, an agent session, a server) the operator or the agent runner learns that a packet was refused when it happens, not at exit: one `denied` record on `--events-fd` per new destination, and a stderr line on request. A runner can then stop, ask for approval or change course while the session is alive.\n\nEvidence. Drop counts are read once, at teardown, and the lifecycle stream (src/cli/event_pipe.rs) has no denial record. The ruleset already keeps the named counter `dropped` and the per-destination sets, and the sandbox side already sends `drops` to the supervisor on the up pipe. The data and the channel exist; only the timing is missing.\n\nFirst slice.\n- The sandbox-side process that reads the counters at teardown also reads the `dropped` counter on a fixed interval (2 s, the pass re-read cadence) and lists the sets only when the counter has moved. It sends new destinations up the existing pipe.\n- The supervisor emits `{\"event\":\"denied\",\"details\":{\"address\",\"protocol\",\"port\",\"packets\"}}` once per new destination. With `--live-drops` it also prints `playpen: dropped: 198.51.100.7 tcp port 80 (not approved)`. Live lines are capped: the first 8 destinations, then one \"more in the final report\" line.\n- The final report still comes from the teardown readback and does not change.\n\nAcceptance.\n- A command that sends to a denied address and then sleeps: the event (and the line, when asked for) appears within one interval plus scheduling slack, while the command is alive. More packets to the same destination produce no second record.\n- A burst to more than 8 destinations stays bounded on stderr and complete in the report.\n- A run with no drops has unchanged output. Without `--live-drops`, attached and `--private-tty` terminals see no new lines.\n- Measure the cost of the interval read with a full 1024-entry set and state it in DESIGN.md. If starting nft every interval is too heavy, read the counter over netlink.\n\nOpen decision. Whether stderr lines should be on by default when stderr is not a terminal. Recommendation: events always, stderr opt-in for the first release.\n\nLater. An NFLOG group read inside the namespace would make this immediate instead of interval-based."}	blake3:P34oTwejhrhYvBXfdPlH8Rj4UnryUJK0D6y9b9E8iU0
1795405821656594	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2kk	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 3 of 8. Needs \"End egress in the firewall itself when the approval ends or is revoked\" for the cut set, and \"Report dropped destinations while the command is still running\" for the stop variant.\n\nUser outcome. An operator who must not leave scope can say `--on-drop cut`: the first packet the firewall refuses also closes every approved destination, in the kernel, with no polling delay. With `--on-drop stop` playpen also ends the command, as it does when a pass is revoked. Today a tool that strays keeps working against the approved targets until it exits, and the warning arrives at the end.\n\nEvidence. Checked on 2026-11-22 in a throwaway namespace (nft 1.1.7): with `meta mark @cut drop` early in the chain and `add @cut { meta mark }` on the refusal path, a ping to the approved address succeeded, one ping to an unapproved address was refused, and the next ping to the approved address was refused too. Later packets stop at the cut rule, so the drop sets hold only what was sent up to the trip and the report can name the destination that tripped it.\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--on-drop cut`: add the self-tripping statement before the drop counter. The command keeps running and sees its connections fail. Report `egress_cut` with the cause, and print which destination tripped it. Packets refused after the trip go to the cut counter, not to the unapproved-destination total.\n- `--on-drop stop`: as cut, and when the supervisor learns of the trip from the live readback it stops the command with the existing SIGTERM, grace, SIGKILL sequence. Report `stopped_by_drop: true` with a `stop_reason`. A stop by drop never exits 0.\n- The option is in `playpen plan`, the digest, the start line and the shareable report.\n\nAcceptance.\n- Fixture with two approved listeners and one unapproved address: requests to the first succeed, one packet goes to the unapproved address, then requests to both approved listeners fail. The listeners' logs agree and the report names the unapproved destination.\n- stop: the command ends within the documented readback interval plus grace, with the specified exit status and report fields.\n- Without the option the ruleset and the report are what they are today.\n- Document what commonly trips it without being an attack: multicast name lookups (mDNS, LLMNR), a client trying a DNS server, a cloud SDK probing the metadata address, a second address family. That is why it is opt-in.\n\nOut of scope. Tripping on Unix-socket refusals or on name lookups."}	blake3:pKBn2ZopngFCdxia90ayOxu37sPORrWYRH1sMlHssfY
1795405821677689	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-bcd	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 4 of 8.\n\nUser outcome. The most common first failure under playpen is \"could not resolve host\", and today nothing records it. A lookup inside the sandbox goes to loopback, is refused there, and is not a dropped packet, so the report is silent (spec: the drop assertion \"counts dropped IP packets, not DNS misses\"). After this change the report lists the names the command asked for and marks the ones with no `--host` mapping, with the advice to add one.\n\nEvidence. src/resolver.rs generates a resolv.conf with no nameserver; resolvers then try 127.0.0.1 port 53, where nothing listens. The ruleset accepts loopback. src/fixture.rs already runs a small listener inside the sandbox's network.\n\nFirst slice.\n- A stub on sandbox loopback, UDP and TCP port 53, bound before the command starts, while the sandbox side can still bind a low port. Write `nameserver 127.0.0.1` into the generated resolv.conf.\n- It reads the question only. For a `--host` name it answers with the mapped A or AAAA records. For every other name it answers that the name does not exist. It forwards nothing, anywhere.\n- It keeps up to 256 distinct names with a count each and sends them up at teardown. Report field `lookups: {\"names\":[{\"name\",\"count\",\"mapped\"}],\"truncated\"}` and a text block: \"The command looked up names that have no --host mapping: ...\".\n- The names come from the workload. Accept only letters, digits, hyphen, underscore and dots, at most 253 bytes; escape everything else before it reaches a terminal or the report.\n\nAcceptance.\n- `getent hosts unmapped.example` fails as fast as it does today, and the report lists the name once with its count.\n- A client that does not read /etc/hosts (`dig`, Node's `dns.resolve`) resolves a `--host` name through the stub.\n- A lookup containing terminal escape sequences, or an over-long label, never reaches the terminal raw.\n- The command cannot bind port 53 and cannot signal or replace the stub.\n- Works with `--no-network`. Not in the shareable report. `playpen compare` treats lookups as observations, like dropped tuples.\n\nOut of scope. Resolving real names. DNS over HTTPS or TLS, which already appears as dropped packets to the resolver's address."}	blake3:crPC_fKWD9yV_icrsXl3647HNkCmt2acTawZSVbORIs
1795405821698811	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1cn	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 5 of 8.\n\nUser outcome. After a run the operator can see each address, port and protocol the command opened a flow to inside the approved scope, and `playpen suggest REPORT` prints the smallest set of `--allow` and `--host` options that would have admitted the same traffic. A wide first grant (a /24, a port range) becomes a reviewed, narrow one from evidence, and an engagement report can list what was touched.\n\nEvidence. `--report-grant-use` counts packets per grant rule, not which hosts inside a network grant were contacted (spec, \"Grant use in private reports\"). The drop path already records address, protocol and port in bounded dynamic sets. Checked on 2026-11-22 in a throwaway namespace (nft 1.1.7): `... add @seen4 { ip daddr . meta l4proto . th dport } accept` on a grant rule recorded three distinct tuples with correct packet counts. It counts only the first packet of each flow, because later packets match the established rule first.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Opt-in `--report-contacts`: sets `seen4` and `seen6` (1024 entries each, the shape of the drop sets), filled on every explicit grant's accept rule, included in the start-up readback check, read at teardown with the drops.\n- Report `contacted: {\"destinations\":[{\"address\",\"protocol\",\"port\",\"packets\"}],\"truncated\"}`. State that `packets` counts flow starts, not volume.\n- `playpen suggest REPORT [--format json]` reads a full report as inert data and prints exact `--allow` lines for the contacted tuples, plus the `--host` entries that point at them. It refuses when the record is truncated or unavailable or the run did not start. It never prints anything wider than the original grants.\n- Works together with `--report-grant-use`. Not allowed with `--shareable-report`.\n\nAcceptance.\n- Fixture with a /24 grant over a port range and three contacted tuples: the report lists exactly those. The `suggest` output, given back to `playpen run`, admits the same requests and drops a fourth.\n- Overflow past 1024 sets `truncated`, and `suggest` refuses.\n- Without the option the ruleset is unchanged.\n- The text output repeats the existing caution: one quiet run does not show that a later run needs less.\n\nOut of scope. A mode that blocks nothing. A deliberately wide `--allow` with this option already serves as a learning run, and it stays an explicit approval."}	blake3:UmmsueHj6xvjjeHg3jFvtncnKt4tQoe33G5PrSUbgyM
1795405821721245	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2h7	{"field":"description","value":"Priority 6 of 8.\n\nUser outcome. An operator can give the owner a record of exactly what was sent. `--capture FILE` writes a pcap of every packet that crossed the sandbox's outside interface, and the report says when the run started and ended, so the capture, the report and the pass window line up.\n\nEvidence. pasta has `-p, --pcap FILE` (\"Log tap-facing traffic to pcap file\"); src/pasta.rs does not pass it. Refused packets are rejected in the output hook before they reach that interface, so the capture should hold approved traffic and its replies only. The test below has to confirm that. The report has no time fields (src/report.rs).\n\nFirst slice.\n- `--capture FILE`: refused with `--no-network`. Create the file safely before pasta starts (new file, mode 0600, no symlink following, never overwrite), and decide whether pasta gets the path or an inherited descriptor.\n- Report: `started_at` and `ended_at` (RFC 3339 UTC; start is when the command is released, end is when it is reaped) in every report of a run that started. `capture: {\"file\",\"bytes\",\"sha256\"}`, computed after pasta has exited.\n- The start line names the capture file. The shareable report never gets the capture fields; whether it gets the two times is an allowlist decision under spec \"Shareable reports\".\n\nAcceptance.\n- Run against a local fixture with one approved and one denied destination: the pcap parses, contains the approved exchange and no packet to the denied address, and its digest matches the report.\n- For `sleep 2`, `ended_at - started_at` matches the run time within a second.\n- An existing file, a symlink and an unwritable directory are refusals before anything runs.\n- A run stopped by its deadline still leaves a complete capture.\n- Docs: the capture holds payloads, including credentials sent in clear. Treat it like the pass. `playpen compare` ignores the new fields."}	blake3:N4OVZSRQXAcPSCDyZ55mN19bigzZcKF2O2p6jJhvglk
1795405821737430	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-jku	{"field":"title","value":"Next capabilities: in-kernel approval end, live denials, lookup names and evidence (idea round, November 2026)"}	blake3:uwa97y1fHdpIidPB_WGOYtOxL1B8VDXJO0RPAKThqhc
1795405821742859	claude	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-jku	{"field":"description","value":"Grouping goal for the ideas kept from a round of one hundred (2026-11-22). Ten were written up; the list was then trimmed with the owner the same day.\n\nBuild, in this order (the 'Priority N of 8' line in each child):\n1. End egress in the firewall itself when the approval ends or is revoked\n2. Report dropped destinations while the command is still running\n3. Tripwire: close all egress at the first packet to an unapproved destination (needs 1 and 2)\n4. Record the names the command tried to look up\n5. Record each destination the command contacted and suggest the narrowest allow list\n6. Keep a packet capture of what left the sandbox, and timestamp the run\n7. Map a pass's own hostnames to their addresses with --pass-hosts (was: approve a name by lookup; the lookup was dropped)\n8. Find out whether the kernel can keep non-IP sockets out of the sandbox (was: a seccomp family filter; reduced to an investigation)\n\nParked: publish a port (punt, until there is a concrete case).\nDropped: plan file (closed; --expect-plan already gives the guarantee).\n\nShared rule for every child: a new option enters playpen plan and the plan digest, the start line and the report, and the published guarantees in README.md, docs/spec.md and docs/DESIGN.md change in the same merge as the code. Close this goal when the children are done or deliberately dropped."}	blake3:OzfYdXc-wwn4kEHGqDryEYnsy9MDQv5nBmWNa_Q6dUA
1795405821759279	claude	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-265	{"reason":"Dropped in the trim of the November 2026 idea round: a convenience only. --expect-plan already binds a run to a reviewed plan, and a plan file would add a new input format to parse, validate and keep compatible.","state":"done"}	blake3:MV_p6T0yklBuyobzDselTtIe5lreUntm1Dc2m8Y-0uU
1795406080266486	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-arr	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:yihkXBiJDtgv-HfMeHJaUw6ZRCWaprhgFepmvIPzvhA
