1795411225013222	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.assign	bn-2kk	{"action":"assign","agent":"playpen-dev"}	blake3:KyE8QUCxEKJAoF5SYkJ0YXr51V9pGYw5gJTnXRzKJos
1795412531529579	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-1cn	{"state":"done"}	blake3:VWniki8aYl2Ati5G2oFoXlX4-HteL1yjeZeDT_OIYn4
1795414325032529	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-2kk	{"state":"done"}	blake3:UBnavYZIAZy4QsmADbqPUDrEx_aKJq6pdfQT-9fCMSU
1795414325059966	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-jku	{"reason":"All eight children done: bn-arr, bn-1cy, bn-2kk, bn-bcd, bn-1cn, bn-2h7, bn-2ja, bn-i89. bn-i89's recommendation is filed as bn-eby (needs an owner decision). bn-gnj stays parked (punt).","state":"done"}	blake3:kLIev1tfL85e_jCD7w34-F3Min1BPfrZ6AMRDh5ptxA
1795452285148017	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3d8	{"description":"An impartial agent used playpen for real work on 2026-11-23 (commit ec4a30f; Arch, Linux 7.2.8, nft 1.1.7, pasta 2026_09_25, Landlock ABI 10). It carried out 22 scenarios with curl, pip, git, nmap, dig, ncat, python, an interactive bash and deliberate escape attempts, and scored playpen 7/10.\n\nWhat held: no packet reached an unapproved destination, every refusal was counted, escape attempts (host loopback, gateway, raw sockets, nft flush, host Unix and abstract sockets, inherited sockets, IPv4-mapped IPv6, multicast) all failed, the kernel end time held while the supervisor was stopped, and the error messages were judged excellent.\n\nWhat cost points: the guarded command can tamper with the files that guard it (its own pass, the --report file); --no-network silently voids the drop-based options; and the tool is heavy to learn (about 3,400 lines of docs, about 30 run options, start lines over 600 characters, refusals that say that something differs but not what).\n\nChildren are ordered by severity; the three marked urgent are the ones the evaluator said keep it from relying on the report and on revocation against a hostile workload. Shared rule, as for bn-jku: a behaviour change updates README.md, docs/spec.md and docs/DESIGN.md in the same merge. Close this goal when the children are done or deliberately dropped.","kind":"goal","labels":["eval-2026-11"],"title":"Close the gaps found by the first hands-on evaluation (score 7/10, November 2026)"}	blake3:_GiR6r3naTgqNTChpW0FrYR9KC_bKwTuzKoCVFiCtZA
1795452285271308	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2sa	{"description":"Priority 1 of 14.\n\nSeverity: security.\n\nUser outcome. An owner who sets revoked = true stops the run, whatever the command does. Today the command can put the old pass back before playpen re-reads it, and the README threat model says the command cannot \"undo the guard\".\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). A pass with mode 0600 and a 10-minute window, run as `playpen run --pass race.pass.toml --pass-hosts -- ./pass-guard.sh race.pass.toml`. The workload copied the pass at start, then every 50 ms compared it with `cmp` and restored the copy with `cp` + `mv -f` if it changed. Three seconds in, the owner ran `sed -i 's/^revoked = false/revoked = true/'`. The workload restored the file 17 ms later; playpen never noticed and the workload made 11 more successful requests over 12 s until the driver killed playpen. Report: stopped_by_pass false. The workload could also read the pass. DESIGN.md says anyone who can write the pass can restore it, but never that the guarded command is such a writer. The kernel end time (bn-arr) still bounds the damage.\n\nFirst slice (to investigate, then decide).\n- Inside the sandbox, bind /dev/null over the pass and its .ledger, as --capture does for the capture file. Check what that leaves open: renaming the parent directory, a hard link to the pass, writing through another path to the same directory.\n- Have the supervisor re-read through a directory descriptor (or O_PATH) held from the start and compare device and inode, so a replaced file is itself a withdrawal (\"the pass file was replaced\").\n- If a sound fix needs the pass outside every directory the command can write, refuse or warn at start when it is not, and say so in the start line.\n- Until fixed, state the limit in the README threat model.\n\nAcceptance.\n- The evaluation's restore loop no longer keeps the run alive: the run stops within the documented interval of the owner's edit, with stopped_by_pass true.\n- The command cannot read the pass.\n- Tests for rename-of-parent and hard-link variants, or a documented reason they cannot happen.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","security","revocation"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"m","title":"Keep the guarded command from rewriting its own pass file and undoing a revocation","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:9lKhUse_N-tk0DFirdn1VxmUJqG79ev4TOeYUOseAnw
1795452285410146	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-33k	{"description":"Priority 2 of 14.\n\nSeverity: security.\n\nUser outcome. A CI job that reads the --report file reads what playpen wrote, not what the command wrote.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). `playpen run --allow 192.0.2.1:443/tcp --fail-on-drop --report ci.json -- ./forge-report.sh ci.json`. The workload sent to 203.0.113.50 tcp/80 and udp/53, then wrote a clean-looking JSON file and `mv -f`'d it over ci.json. playpen's exit status (1) and stderr warning were right, but the file on disk said playpen_exit_status 0, drop_assertion \"pass\", 0 dropped. `playpen compare` against a baseline with drops exited 0, with no warning. DESIGN.md mentions that the command could replace the path; the README CI example writes `--report report.json` into the working directory.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Inside the sandbox, bind /dev/null over the report path, as --capture does.\n- At teardown, compare fstat of the descriptor opened at start with stat of the path. If they differ, write the real report to a temporary file and rename it into place, warn on stderr, and make playpen's exit status non-zero.\n- Move the README CI example's report outside the workspace, and say why.\n\nAcceptance.\n- The evaluation's forge script leaves the real report on disk (2 dropped, violated) and a warning on stderr.\n- The command sees /dev/null at the report path.\n- Rename-of-parent and symlink variants are tested or documented.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","security","reporting","ci"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"s","title":"Keep the guarded command from replacing the --report file with a forged one","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:4k36-oHNQBDEqjIepWxh5MwXfmEakW_kC7UWS7WVH5Y
1795452285549491	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-17z	{"description":"Priority 3 of 14.\n\nSeverity: bug (a false pass in CI).\n\nUser outcome. A build that tries to phone home fails the CI check with --no-network, as it does with a network. Today it passes.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). A build script that runs `curl http://203.0.113.50/` and `ncat -u 203.0.113.50 8125`:\n- `playpen run --no-network --fail-on-drop --report r.json -- ./sneaky-build.sh` exits 0, prints \"no packets to unapproved destinations were dropped\", drop_assertion \"pass\".\n- With `--on-drop stop --live-drops` added, the start line promises a stop that never comes.\n- With `--allow 192.0.2.1:443/tcp` instead, the same build gives 2 dropped packets and exit 1.\nCause: with only lo, connect fails with ENETUNREACH at routing, before the output hook sees a packet. The spec mentions packets that \"never reached the ruleset\" only in a corner of section 4.5; neither the README CI section nor --help mentions it.\n\nFirst slice (choose one, record the choice in DESIGN.md).\n- Preferred: give the --no-network sandbox a dummy interface with IPv4 and IPv6 default routes, so these packets reach the filter and are counted and refused like any other. Check that nothing can leave through a dummy device and that the doctor's offline checks still hold.\n- Otherwise: refuse --fail-on-drop, --on-drop and --live-drops with --no-network, or report drop_assertion \"unknown\" with a message.\n\nAcceptance.\n- The evaluation's sneaky build fails the CI check under --no-network (or is refused before running).\n- --on-drop stop under --no-network stops the build at its first attempt, or is refused.\n- The README CI section and --help say what is counted.\n\nRelated: the IPv6 no-route bone (same root cause).","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","bug","ci","observability"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"m","title":"Make --fail-on-drop, --on-drop and --live-drops see refused attempts under --no-network","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:PAFxXTRh7i6bIwWnYCsmON1rJ47GxMZONnMmO__doTw
1795452285693384	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-q4y	{"description":"Priority 4 of 14.\n\nSeverity: bug. Same root cause as the --no-network false-pass bone: a connect that fails at routing never reaches the ruleset.\n\nUser outcome. An IPv6 attempt to an unapproved address is refused and reported on every host, so the same CI check gives the same answer on a host with IPv6 and on one without.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). Host with IPv4 plus IPv6 ULA addresses only, no IPv6 default route. `playpen run --allow 172.66.147.243:443/tcp --host example.com=172.66.147.243 --report r.json -- sh -c 'curl -6 https://[2606:4700:10::ac42:93f3]/; ...'`: curl fails and only the IPv4 drops are listed. Inside the sandbox there is no IPv6 default route (pasta copies the host's routes). The README says IPv6 TCP gets \"Permission denied\" and is reported.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Add a catch-all IPv6 (and IPv4, when the host has none) default route into pasta's interface when the host lacks one, so attempts reach the filter. Check what pasta does with an approved IPv6 packet it cannot forward.\n- Otherwise flag \"no IPv6 route: IPv6 attempts are not counted\" on the start line and in the report, and make --fail-on-drop say \"unknown\" for such runs.\n\nAcceptance.\n- On a host without an IPv6 default route, `curl -6` to an unapproved address is counted in dropped.\n- Approved IPv4 traffic is unchanged; the doctor reports the route state.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","bug","ipv6","observability"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"m","title":"Count IPv6 attempts on hosts without an IPv6 default route"}	blake3:J5hnH_bo7Xn5OqsmrwPHnd6EGhLwGdFQyHDMGBAX1j4
1795452285834090	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-39y	{"description":"Priority 5 of 14.\n\nSeverity: bug.\n\nUser outcome. The report's stop booleans (also in the shareable report) say the same thing for the same event.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). A 12 s pass with a polling client gives stopped_by_until true, stop_reason \"the approval ended at ...\". The same run with playpen sent SIGSTOP 4 s before the end and SIGCONT 7 s after gives stopped_by_until false, stopped_by_pass true, stop_reason \"the pass no longer permits anything (expired): ...\". On resume the parent loop re-reads the pass before it checks the deadline (src/sandbox.rs, `parent`), and hallpass reports the pass as expired.\n\nFirst slice. Check the deadline before the pass re-read on every loop turn, or map a hallpass `expired` result that coincides with the run's end time to the until-stop. Say in the spec which one wins.\n\nAcceptance. A test that stops playpen across the end time (tests/egress_end.rs has one) asserts stopped_by_until true and stopped_by_pass false.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","bug","reporting"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"xs","title":"Classify an expiry the same way whether or not the supervisor was paused"}	blake3:5tkUcdf5vDArrbb5kpuOIwqJpXcVXYFhzmD1jXjAyu4
1795452286010474	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3oo	{"description":"Priority 6 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability.\n\nUser outcome. An operator running a pass with [ceilings] learns at start that playpen does not enforce them, as they already learn for destination `names`.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). A pass with `[ceilings] requests = 100` and a destination with `names`: the start line has a NOTE for names, nothing for ceilings. Only --require-enforced reveals it, and its message does not name the ceiling.\n\nFirst slice. Print \"NOTE: the pass's ceilings (requests, ...) are not enforced by playpen; an application-level enforcer must apply them\" after the start line, listing the ceiling keys present. Same wording style as the names NOTE.\n\nAcceptance. A test pass with two ceilings prints one NOTE naming both; a pass without ceilings prints none.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","approval"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"xs","title":"Print a NOTE when a pass has ceilings that playpen does not enforce"}	blake3:oO69cIBRwe7c8lLTvOA0uWdwSkohzrlHEAOMZCfiei0
1795452286159097	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-kih	{"description":"Priority 7 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability.\n\nUser outcome. When playpen says that two things differ, it says what differs, so the operator does not have to diff JSON by hand.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23).\n- `playpen compare wide.json narrow.json` prints only \"policy changed\" and exits 2; the JSON has only \"policy\":\"changed\", with no added or removed grants.\n- An --expect-plan mismatch prints only the two digests.\n- --require-enforced prints \"selected names or application ceilings require an external enforcer\" without naming the destination (`stats`) or the ceiling (`requests`), though the report's enforcement_coverage has both.\n\nFirst slice.\n- compare: add `policy_changes` (added and removed allowed specs, host mappings, network, socket guard, exposed paths) to JSON and text.\n- --expect-plan: name the plan fields that differ (requires keeping the reviewed plan, or printing the current plan's fields next to the digest; decide).\n- --require-enforced: name the labels and ceilings.\n\nAcceptance. Each of the three cases above prints the specific difference; existing exit codes are unchanged.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","review"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"s","title":"Say what differs: compare policy changes, --expect-plan mismatches and --require-enforced refusals"}	blake3:MCuSJNwTq7LgQT73ZpSwfaPGJoNYJ6fe8ScxbY0z-0U
1795452286291929	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-cj6	{"description":"Priority 8 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability and docs.\n\nUser outcome. An operator writing a pass by hand can read every problem, and the fix it suggests is a command they have.\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). `playpen rules --pass bad.pass.toml` with 7 deliberate mistakes: all 7 problems are joined with \"; \" on one line of about 1,500 characters. The message says \"then run `hallpass check`\", but no hallpass binary is installed and playpen does not ship one; PASS-FORMAT.md (hallpass) also points to `hallpass init` and `hallpass check`. The problems themselves are excellent (each has a field path and what to write).\n\nFirst slice.\n- Text output: one problem per line, indented under one heading. JSON output: an array of problems.\n- Replace the `hallpass check` pointer with a check playpen has: `playpen rules --pass FILE`, or a new `playpen check-pass FILE` (decide; a new command must go through help, discovery and the spec).\n- Consider a pass template (`playpen pass-template` or a documented example file), since writing a pass by hand was part of the friction.\n\nAcceptance. The 7-mistake pass prints 7 lines; the suggested command exists and succeeds on a valid pass.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","docs","approval"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"s","title":"Print invalid-pass problems one per line and point to a check that playpen ships"}	blake3:QTh1bvRAKXW8y639gy73i-Q9o4QBC3LimzUDgXuFuJs
1795452286442372	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3co	{"description":"Priority 9 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability (low).\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). `playpen run --events-fd 3 ... 3>events.jsonl` is refused: \"requires the write end of a pipe; nothing was run\". Writing events to a file is what an agent runner tries first.\n\nFirst slice. Decide whether a regular file opened for writing (append) is safe to accept: the pipe requirement exists so one write <= PIPE_BUF is atomic and a slow reader cannot block the supervisor; a regular file never blocks. If accepted, document it. If not, the refusal shows the workaround: `3> >(cat > events.jsonl)`.\n\nAcceptance. `3>events.jsonl` either works (records in the file) or the error message shows a working workaround.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","agents"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"xs","title":"Let --events-fd write to a regular file, or show the workaround in its refusal"}	blake3:wwjYI0PLsfUf7R-u26rL7rNECwNef2dAcCGqjU0X398
1795452286620462	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-np3	{"description":"Priority 10 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability (low).\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). Under --fail-on-drop the log ends with \"command exited with status 0\" and the warning block, then playpen exits 1 with no line saying why. --on-drop stop prints an \"exiting with status 1: ...\" line; --fail-on-drop does not.\n\nFirst slice. Print \"playpen: exiting with status 1: --fail-on-drop and N packet(s) to unapproved destinations were dropped\" (and the unknown-counter variant). Spec: add it to the stderr messages list.\n\nAcceptance. A test asserts the line for violated and unknown, and its absence for pass.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","ci"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"xs","title":"Say why playpen exits 1 under --fail-on-drop"}	blake3:kqc2pTwy1OdWst_3u8cXA6E8bkGIRlYWpz8EEa2vLys
1795452286830096	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2z9	{"description":"Priority 11 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability (low).\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). In a wide run the client tried 198.51.100.9:8080, which had no listener and timed out. `playpen suggest` printed `--allow 198.51.100.9:8080/tcp` alongside the working destinations, with no mark. A reviewer narrowing a grant would keep a destination that was never used successfully.\n\nFirst slice. Find out whether the contacted record can tell answered flows from unanswered ones (for example a second set filled from the input chain's established/related accept, or a conntrack state match on replies), and what that costs in the ruleset and the readback. If cheap, add `answered` per destination and have suggest mark or separate unanswered ones (still printed, never silently dropped). If not, say in suggest's caution that the list includes attempts that got no answer.\n\nAcceptance. The evaluation's case shows 198.51.100.9:8080 marked as unanswered, or the caution says why it cannot be told.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","review"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"s","title":"Mark suggested destinations that never answered"}	blake3:v6uGMKoKF5mYUWXjco2p72n8DRF-Mk-YEHpQVv559rI
1795452287133439	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ta	{"description":"Priority 12 of 14.\n\nSeverity: docs (low).\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). Following the README \"Reach a test service on this machine\" recipe and adding an IPv6 address to svc0 but none to host0: pasta copies svc0's IPv6 address into the sandbox, so the IPv6 service is the sandbox's own address and cannot be reached. playpen's NOTE explains it well, but the recipe should not lead there.\n\nFirst slice. Extend the recipe with `ip addr add 2001:db8:1::10/64 dev host0 nodad; ip -6 route add default via 2001:db8:1::1 dev host0` (worked in the evaluation) and an IPv6 service address on svc0, with one sentence on why host0 needs its own IPv6 address.\n\nAcceptance. The recipe, copied as written, reaches both an IPv4 and an IPv6 service.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","docs","ipv6"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"xs","title":"README test-service recipe: show how to add an IPv6 service"}	blake3:pfOUDJMtwO8CBXHfDDXq2LR3DQjmxKzThbvOFi75DC0
1795452287264293	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-m7e	{"description":"Priority 13 of 14.\n\nSeverity: usability (low). Each item is small; split any that grows.\n\n- A nested playpen fails with \"cannot make mounts private: EPERM\". Say plainly that playpen cannot run inside playpen (the README already says so).\n- Start lines under a pass exceed 600 characters, hard to read in a CI log. Consider wrapping into a short first line plus indented detail lines in text mode (keep JSON as is), or a --quiet-start.\n- Awkward message: \"includes IPv4-mapped IPv6 (write the IPv4 address instead) addresses\". Reword.\n- `--allow 169.254.169.254:80/tcp` is accepted while a pass refuses link-local addresses. Decide on one rule (at least a NOTE that this is the cloud metadata address).\n- `--host a.test=[192.0.2.1]` (an IPv4 address in brackets) is accepted. Refuse brackets around IPv4.\n- `--hide` paths are missing from the start line, while `--expose` paths are shown. Show both.\n\nAcceptance. Each item fixed or explicitly declined in this bone with a reason.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","usability","docs"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"s","title":"Small rough edges from the November 2026 evaluation"}	blake3:js9mqPx_x-08Yi1KbYV06mpy-gUdyxN1yYb7WYubGFc
1795452287419217	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1c8	{"description":"Priority 14 of 14.\n\nSeverity: test hygiene (not a product bug as far as the evaluation could reproduce).\n\nEvidence (evaluation 2026-11-23). Five `playpen run` process trees from target/debug and .maw/workspaces/*/target/debug were still running, up to 3 days 23 hours old. They come from --private-tty and --new-session tests. One (pid 1954525 at the time) held /dev/ptmx as fd 3, which suggests the test harness leaked the pty master into the child. A --private-tty run by hand ended cleanly when its terminal went away.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Find the tests that start these trees (tests/terminal.rs is the likely source) and make the harness close the pty master in the child and kill the test's process group on timeout or panic (a guard with Drop).\n- Possibly related: the known flaky `private_tty_inner_job_control_keeps_the_outer_output_pipeline_running` (about 1 in 5 on main, more often from a workspace path). Check whether leftover trees or leaked descriptors explain it.\n\nAcceptance. After `cargo test` (including a forced timeout of one terminal test), no playpen process from the test binaries remains.","kind":"task","labels":["eval-2026-11","tests","hygiene"],"parent":"bn-3d8","size":"s","title":"Stop test runs from leaving orphaned playpen processes"}	blake3:c7wmjY2Tm4jSl3mKjKrPC5kPymnGh0Dwoxi_rllYc3Y
