1795456473019892	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-17z	{"reason":"under --no-network a discard0 interface carries v4/v6 default routes; the sneaky build now fails --fail-on-drop (violated, 2 packets); tests/routes.rs","state":"done"}	blake3:bIodUJfnwzxEWyjbQ8osGUFFEZ6foV6JGvj0IAbQpqU
1795456492490146	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-e3h	{"description":"Found while fixing bn-1c8 (2026-11-23).\n\nUser outcome. When the shell that started `playpen run` dies, the run ends, even if it was stopped with Ctrl-Z at the time.\n\nEvidence. An attached (no --private-tty, no --new-session) run stopped with Ctrl-Z, whose shell is then SIGKILLed, stays stopped forever: P sits in state T. The kernel never sends SIGHUP+SIGCONT for an orphaned process group, because P's group never counts as orphaned: the command in it has I (another process group in the same session) as its parent. A real terminal close is fine, because bash itself sends SIGHUP to its jobs. Reproduced by the bn-1c8 agent with an injected failing test and the old test-harness Drop (3 of 4 injected cases left trees; this was one).\n\nFirst slice. Decide whether P should notice that its controlling terminal or its session leader is gone while stopped (for example a watchdog in I that sees P stopped and the terminal hung up), and what it should do: continue and end the command as for a hangup. Check against the SIGSTOP freeze design (I freezes the sandbox when P is silent for 5 s).\n\nAcceptance. A test that stops an attached run with Ctrl-Z, kills its shell, and sees the whole tree end within a bounded time; or a documented reason this cannot be done.","kind":"task","labels":["bug","terminal","follow-up"],"size":"s","title":"End an attached run that stays stopped after its shell is killed"}	blake3:bbqbNPZs0JDLxTsi4TvVQo2uZIiuFB6y1ZmQLEZXIJg
1795456492585210	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1e5	{"description":"Found while fixing bn-1c8 (2026-11-23).\n\nEvidence. tests/capture.rs `a_run_stopped_by_its_deadline_leaves_a_complete_capture` fails if any process on the host has \"deadline.pcap\" in its command line. So a second workspace running the same test at the same time trips it. It failed once in the bn-1c8 agent's loaded runs.\n\nFirst slice. Give the capture file a name unique to the test run (pid plus a counter, under a per-test temp directory), and match on that full path, or find the processes by session or parent instead of by command line.\n\nAcceptance. The test passes while a copy of itself runs in another workspace.","kind":"task","labels":["tests","flaky","follow-up"],"size":"xs","title":"Stop the capture deadline test from matching other workspaces' runs"}	blake3:DxeoX-YPd8G6rUw3DNQCUsC0q4ac6purRmbssZ8KtbQ
1795456492676777	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-321	{"description":"Found while fixing bn-1c8 (2026-11-23). bn-1c8 fixed tests/terminal.rs only.\n\nEvidence. Other test files spawn `playpen run` children that a panic before the explicit kill would leave running, for example tests/run.rs `killing_playpen_kills_the_sandbox`, whose command loops forever.\n\nFirst slice. Audit tests/*.rs for spawned children that run until killed, and wrap them in a guard whose Drop kills the child's process group or session (the same approach as `Pty`'s Drop in tests/terminal.rs, `session_and_descendants`), then reaps. Consider moving that helper into tests/common.\n\nAcceptance. With a panic injected after each such spawn, no process from the test binaries remains after `cargo test`.","kind":"task","labels":["tests","hygiene","follow-up"],"size":"s","title":"Kill the playpen children of integration tests when a test panics"}	blake3:Vxa719pSbMbDQTTK38WchwtJwMkfUQQbjNE-9Cnwgbc
