1795457010050273	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-r5m	{"description":"Found while fixing bn-2sa (2026-11-23).\n\nUser outcome. An owner who edits the pass with sed -i or an editor, while the pass's directory is not hidden, can still trust that the command cannot read or replace the new pass.\n\nEvidence. bn-2sa binds /dev/null over the pass and its ledger inside the sandbox. A rename over the pass on the host (sed -i, most editors) detaches that bind in every mount namespace, so the new pass becomes visible inside. A command that can write the directory can then read it, or rename an unrevoked copy over it before the next re-read. The bn-2sa agent measured this: a command that rewrites `revoked = true` back to `false` kept a revoked run alive when the directory was not hidden. With the pass in its own directory and `--hide DIR`, the attack fails (tests/own_files.rs). Today playpen prints a start NOTE when the directory is not hidden, and the README example uses `--hide ~/passes`.\n\nFirst slice (to investigate, then decide).\n- Have I (inside the mount namespace) bind /dev/null over the pass again whenever it changes. Measure the window between the owner's rename and the new bind, and decide whether any window is acceptable.\n- Or refuse, behind an option such as --require-hidden-pass, a pass whose directory the command can write and that is not hidden. Consider making that the default for new runs.\n- Or hide the pass's directory automatically when it neither is nor contains the working directory. Check what that hides by accident: home, ~/.config.\n\nAcceptance. The bn-2sa agent's un-revoke attack, run against an unhidden directory, fails or is refused before the run; or a documented decision says why the NOTE is the limit.","kind":"task","labels":["security","revocation","follow-up"],"size":"m","title":"Keep an edited pass out of the command's reach when its directory is not hidden"}	blake3:ctffR4oT5iMvFI4dEljAznNYut0t7plASRWRsqEVjTg
