1795464716553412	playpen-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1os	{"description":"Found while fixing bn-e3h (2026-11-23).\n\nUser outcome. playpen never panics because its terminal went away or a reader closed a pipe early. It keeps supervising, tearing down and writing its report, and it exits with its normal status.\n\nEvidence. playpen writes its human-readable messages with `eprintln!`/`eprint!` (about 34 call sites, mostly src/cli.rs, plus src/sandbox.rs, src/suggest.rs, src/landlock.rs and src/cli/discovery.rs) and its stdout output with `print!`/`println!`. These macros panic when the write fails:\n- With EIO on stderr after the terminal emulator closes the pty master, P panics mid-run on its next notice, for example the end-of-run summary after a hangup.\n- With EPIPE on stdout, for example `playpen rules | head -1` or `playpen suggest r.json | head -1`, it panics with \"failed printing to stdout: Broken pipe\". Rust ignores SIGPIPE by default.\n\nFirst slice.\n- Route every stderr message through a helper that writes and ignores the error.\n- For stdout: when the write fails with EPIPE, stop writing and exit with the status the command would have had (or 0 for a listing command). Never panic. Decide and document the exit status.\n- Add a clippy `disallowed_macros` entry (clippy.toml) for `eprintln`, `eprint`, `println` and `print` in non-test code, so no new call site comes back.\n- Tests: `playpen rules --format json | head -c 1` exits without a panic message on stderr; a run whose stderr is a closed pipe completes and writes its report file.\n\nAcceptance. No \"failed printing\" panic is reachable. The tests above pass.","kind":"task","labels":["bug","robustness","follow-up"],"size":"s","title":"Never panic when stderr or stdout cannot be written"}	blake3:eW36zAl20RDogx5kLLkU6j2wlGH55s_OxqExDQuV8VA
